/* notes for jpn - generated by tools/build_site.py */
window.COURSE.notes = window.COURSE.notes || {};
window.COURSE.notes["jpn"] = [{"n": 1, "title": "Kanji & Kana Foundations", "tagline": "Read hiragana and katakana fluently, and recognise about 80 basic kanji with their readings.", "minutes": 16, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Japanese runs on three scripts at the same time, and JLPT N5 expects you to handle all three without freezing. <strong>Hiragana</strong> (ひらがな, hiragana) is the rounded script used for grammar endings and native words. <strong>Katakana</strong> (カタカナ, katakana) is the angular script used for foreign words and names. <strong>Kanji</strong> (漢字, kanji) are meaning-carrying characters borrowed from Chinese; your syllabus asks for about 80 of them.</p>\n<p>A useful picture: a Japanese sentence is a train. The kanji are the cargo wagons, they tell you what the sentence is about. The hiragana are the couplings and signal lamps, they show who did what to whom. Learn only the kanji and you will know the nouns but not the sentence. That is exactly why some students \"know the kanji\" and still cannot read a line.</p>\n<p>The good news is that kana is small and closed. 46 basic hiragana, 46 basic katakana, and every other kana shape you meet is a small variation on those 92.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why this order</span><p>Kana is finite and it unlocks everything else. Two weeks of honest kana drill lets you read vocabulary lists, grammar notes and the answer options on a question paper. Skipping kana to \"do kanji first\" is the single most common way beginners stall.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>Japanese is written in units called <em>mora</em> (モーラ, mōra). A mora is one beat, and almost every mora is a consonant plus one of five vowels. The five vowels are あ (a), い (i), う (u), え (e), お (o), always in that order.</p>\n<p>Say them short and flat, closer to Spanish or Italian than to English. あ (a) as in \"father\" but cut short. い (i) as in \"bit\". う (u) as in \"put\", with lips relaxed, not rounded like \"you\". え (e) as in \"bet\". お (o) as in \"or\" but short. Length matters: a long vowel is a different word, so おばさん (obasan) is aunt and おばあさん (obaasan) is grandmother.</p>\n<p><strong>Romaji</strong> (ローマ字, rōmaji) is Japanese written in Latin letters. Treat it as training wheels. It is how these notes show you the sound, but the exam is written in kana and kanji, so never study romaji as if it were the target.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Do not read romaji with English vowels. <code>a</code> is never the \"a\" of \"cat\", and <code>u</code> is never the \"u\" of \"but\". Say the five vowels aloud every study session until they come out flat and even.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.1", "title": "Hiragana, the grammar script", "html": "<p>Hiragana is 46 base characters arranged in the <em>gojūon</em> (五十音, gojūon, \"fifty sounds\") grid. Read the table left to right, vowel by vowel. The ん (n) is the one mora with no vowel.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>a</th><th>i</th><th>u</th><th>e</th><th>o</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>-</td><td>あ a</td><td>い i</td><td>う u</td><td>え e</td><td>お o</td></tr>\n<tr><td>k</td><td>か ka</td><td>き ki</td><td>く ku</td><td>け ke</td><td>こ ko</td></tr>\n<tr><td>s</td><td>さ sa</td><td>し shi</td><td>す su</td><td>せ se</td><td>そ so</td></tr>\n<tr><td>t</td><td>た ta</td><td>ち chi</td><td>つ tsu</td><td>て te</td><td>と to</td></tr>\n<tr><td>n</td><td>な na</td><td>に ni</td><td>ぬ nu</td><td>ね ne</td><td>の no</td></tr>\n<tr><td>h</td><td>は ha</td><td>ひ hi</td><td>ふ fu</td><td>へ he</td><td>ほ ho</td></tr>\n<tr><td>m</td><td>ま ma</td><td>み mi</td><td>む mu</td><td>め me</td><td>も mo</td></tr>\n<tr><td>y</td><td>や ya</td><td>-</td><td>ゆ yu</td><td>-</td><td>よ yo</td></tr>\n<tr><td>r</td><td>ら ra</td><td>り ri</td><td>る ru</td><td>れ re</td><td>ろ ro</td></tr>\n<tr><td>w</td><td>わ wa</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>を o</td></tr>\n<tr><td>n</td><td colspan=\"5\">ん n</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Four additions cover almost everything else you will read.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Dakuten</strong> (゛) voices a consonant: か (ka) becomes が (ga), さ (sa) becomes ざ (za), た (ta) becomes だ (da), は (ha) becomes ば (ba).</li>\n<li><strong>Handakuten</strong> (゜) exists only on the h-row and makes a p: は (ha) becomes ぱ (pa).</li>\n<li><strong>Small っ</strong> doubles the next consonant and adds a beat of silence: きって (kitte) is a postage stamp, きて (kite) is \"come\".</li>\n<li><strong>Small ゃ ゅ ょ</strong> glue onto an i-row kana to make one sound: き + ゃ is きゃ (kya), し + ょ is しょ (sho) as in しょくじ (shokuji) meaning meal.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two kana change pronunciation only when used as grammar particles. は (ha) is read <em>wa</em>, を (o) is read <em>o</em>, and へ (he) is read <em>e</em>. Written は, spoken wa.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.2", "title": "Katakana, the loanword script", "html": "<p>Katakana carries exactly the same 46 sounds as hiragana, with different shapes. Same sound, different job. You use it for words borrowed from other languages, for foreign place names and people, for sound effects, and sometimes for emphasis the way English uses italics.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Katakana</th><th>Romaji</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>テレビ</td><td>terebi</td><td>television</td></tr>\n<tr><td>パン</td><td>pan</td><td>bread</td></tr>\n<tr><td>カメラ</td><td>kamera</td><td>camera</td></tr>\n<tr><td>コーヒー</td><td>kōhī</td><td>coffee</td></tr>\n<tr><td>パソコン</td><td>pasokon</td><td>personal computer</td></tr>\n<tr><td>インド</td><td>Indo</td><td>India</td></tr>\n<tr><td>カレー</td><td>karē</td><td>curry</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Three habits make katakana readable fast.</p>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">1</span><div><p>Watch the long vowel mark ー. It stretches the vowel of the syllable before it, so コーヒー (kōhī) is ko-o-hi-i, four beats, not two.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">2</span><div><p>Watch small ッ, which doubles the next consonant: カップ (kappu) is a cup, and the small ッ is a full silent beat.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">3</span><div><p>Watch small ャ ュ ョ and the vowel pairs, which let Japanese spell foreign sounds: キャ (kya), ティ (ti), ファ (fa).</p></div></div></div>\n<p>Look-alikes are the real danger. シ (shi) and ツ (tsu) differ in the direction of the two small strokes. ソ (so) and ン (n) differ the same way. ク (ku) and ワ (wa) differ only in the angle of the top stroke. Slow down on those four and you will not misread words.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.3", "title": "What a kanji actually gives you", "html": "<p>A kanji is a package of three things: a meaning, a shape, and usually two kinds of reading. English letters record sound only. A kanji records meaning first, and you supply the sound from context. That is why the same character can be said differently in different words.</p>\n<p>The two readings are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>On-yomi</strong> (音読み, on-yomi), the Chinese-derived reading, normally used inside compounds of two or more kanji. 山 (yama) reads サン (san) in 富士山 (Fujisan), Mount Fuji.</li>\n<li><strong>Kun-yomi</strong> (訓読み, kun-yomi), the native Japanese reading, normally used when the kanji stands alone or with hiragana attached. 山 alone is やま (yama), mountain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>You do not memorise readings as a list. You memorise <em>words</em>, and the readings come along. 日本 (nihon, Japan) teaches you that 日 can be にち and 本 can be ほん, and 日本人 (nihonjin, Japanese person) then costs you almost nothing.</p>\n<p><strong>Radicals</strong> (部首, bushu) are reusable components, and they are your best memory hook. The water radical 氵 appears in 池 (ike, pond), 海 (umi, sea) and 酒 (sake, alcohol). The person radical 亻 appears in 休 (yasumu, to rest), which is a person leaning against a tree. You are not memorising 80 random drawings, you are learning a small set of parts that repeat.</p>\n<p>Stroke order is not decoration. Write in this order: top to bottom, left to right, horizontal before vertical, left-falling stroke before right-falling, and finish the enclosing box before you seal it. Correct order is what makes your handwriting readable and your recall faster.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "1.4", "title": "The 80-kanji set, sampled", "html": "<p>Your syllabus sets the target at roughly 80 basic kanji, read and written. Nobody learns 80 in a day, so learn them in meaning groups: nature, people, time, numbers, actions. Here is a sample of the core set with one common reading each. Note that the reading given is the one you will meet first, not the only one.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kanji</th><th>Reading</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>日</td><td>ひ (hi) / にち (nichi)</td><td>day, sun</td></tr>\n<tr><td>月</td><td>つき (tsuki) / げつ (getsu)</td><td>moon, month</td></tr>\n<tr><td>火</td><td>ひ (hi) / か (ka)</td><td>fire</td></tr>\n<tr><td>水</td><td>みず (mizu) / すい (sui)</td><td>water</td></tr>\n<tr><td>木</td><td>き (ki) / もく (moku)</td><td>tree, wood</td></tr>\n<tr><td>金</td><td>かね (kane) / きん (kin)</td><td>money, gold</td></tr>\n<tr><td>土</td><td>つち (tsuchi) / ど (do)</td><td>earth, soil</td></tr>\n<tr><td>人</td><td>ひと (hito) / じん (jin)</td><td>person</td></tr>\n<tr><td>大</td><td>おおきい (ōkii) / だい (dai)</td><td>big</td></tr>\n<tr><td>小</td><td>ちいさい (chiisai) / しょう (shō)</td><td>small</td></tr>\n<tr><td>中</td><td>なか (naka) / ちゅう (chū)</td><td>inside, middle</td></tr>\n<tr><td>上</td><td>うえ (ue) / じょう (jō)</td><td>up, above</td></tr>\n<tr><td>下</td><td>した (shita) / か (ka)</td><td>down, below</td></tr>\n<tr><td>山</td><td>やま (yama) / さん (san)</td><td>mountain</td></tr>\n<tr><td>川</td><td>かわ (kawa) / せん (sen)</td><td>river</td></tr>\n<tr><td>口</td><td>くち (kuchi) / こう (kō)</td><td>mouth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>目</td><td>め (me) / もく (moku)</td><td>eye</td></tr>\n<tr><td>手</td><td>て (te) / しゅ (shu)</td><td>hand</td></tr>\n<tr><td>学</td><td>がく (gaku) / まなぶ (manabu)</td><td>study, learning</td></tr>\n<tr><td>校</td><td>こう (kō)</td><td>school</td></tr>\n<tr><td>生</td><td>せい (sei) / いきる (ikiru)</td><td>life, birth</td></tr>\n<tr><td>年</td><td>とし (toshi) / ねん (nen)</td><td>year</td></tr>\n<tr><td>時</td><td>とき (toki) / じ (ji)</td><td>time, hour</td></tr>\n<tr><td>間</td><td>あいだ (aida) / かん (kan)</td><td>interval, between</td></tr>\n<tr><td>今</td><td>いま (ima) / こん (kon)</td><td>now</td></tr>\n<tr><td>食</td><td>たべる (taberu) / しょく (shoku)</td><td>eat, food</td></tr>\n<tr><td>見</td><td>みる (miru) / けん (ken)</td><td>see, look</td></tr>\n<tr><td>聞</td><td>きく (kiku) / ぶん (bun)</td><td>hear, ask</td></tr>\n<tr><td>行</td><td>いく (iku) / こう (kō)</td><td>go</td></tr>\n<tr><td>来</td><td>くる (kuru) / らい (rai)</td><td>come</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>N5 does not ask \"list the readings of 日\". It shows a word in kana and asks you to pick the right kanji, or shows a kanji word and asks for its kana reading. So practise in both directions, and always inside a real word.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Rules that never change", "html": "<p>These four rules explain most of the kana spelling you will ever see.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">small っ = one silent beat + doubled next consonant</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The small っ is a full mora of silence that then doubles the consonant after it.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Whenever you hear a short sharp stop in the middle of a word.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> きって (kitte) postage stamp, がっこう (gakkō) school, いってきます (ittekimasu) I am off.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">long vowel = あ / い / う after a vowel of the same row</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> A second vowel of the same row stretches the sound instead of adding a new syllable.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> a-row and o-row usually take あ, i-row takes い, u-row takes う.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> おかあさん (okaasan) mother, おにいさん (oniisan) elder brother, くうこう (kūkō) airport.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">dakuten voices the k, s, t, h rows</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Two small strokes change an unvoiced consonant into its voiced partner.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> k to g, s to z, t to d, h to b, and h to p with the handakuten circle.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> か (ka) to が (ga) in がくせい (gakusei) student, は (ha) to ば (ba) in ばん (ban) evening.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">particle spellings: は = wa, へ = e, を = o</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Three kana keep their old spelling but changed pronunciation when used as particles.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Only as particles. Inside a word they keep their normal sound.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> わたし は がくせい です (watashi wa gakusei desu) I am a student.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Combined kana</th><th>Romaji</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>きゃ きゅ きょ</td><td>kya kyu kyo</td><td>きょう (kyō) today</td></tr>\n<tr><td>しゃ しゅ しょ</td><td>sha shu sho</td><td>しゃしん (shashin) photograph</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ちゃ ちゅ ちょ</td><td>cha chu cho</td><td>おちゃ (ocha) tea</td></tr>\n<tr><td>にゃ にゅ にょ</td><td>nya nyu nyo</td><td>にゅうがく (nyūgaku) school admission</td></tr>\n<tr><td>じゃ じゅ じょ</td><td>ja ju jo</td><td>じしょ (jisho) dictionary</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — reading a hiragana word</h4>\n<p>Word: がっこう. Break it into mora: が (ga) + っ (silent beat, doubles the next consonant) + こ (ko) + う (u, long vowel). So it is ga-k-ko-o, four beats.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">がっこう (gakkō) — school</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — reading katakana with a long vowel mark</h4>\n<p>Word: コーヒー. Mora: コ (ko) + ー (stretch) + ヒ (hi) + ー (stretch). Four beats, not two. English speakers usually clip this to \"coffee\" with two beats, which sounds wrong to a Japanese ear.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">コーヒー (kōhī) — coffee</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 3 — kanji readings inside a compound</h4>\n<p>日本語 is three kanji: 日 (nichi) + 本 (hon) + 語 (go, language). All three take on-yomi because it is a compound. Add 人 (jin, person) and you get 日本人 (nihonjin), a Japanese person, same readings again.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">日本語 (nihongo) — Japanese language; 日本人 (nihonjin) — Japanese person</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 4 — a full sentence in all three scripts</h4>\n<p>わたし は インド じん です。The kanji-free version is all hiragana and katakana: わたし (watashi, I) は (wa, topic) インド (Indo, India) じん (jin, person) です (desu, am). Written properly it is 私はインド人です。</p>\n<div class=\"res\">わたし は インドじん です (watashi wa Indo-jin desu) — I am Indian.</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Writing the topic particle as わ. It is pronounced wa but always written は. わたし わ がくせい です is wrong on paper even though it sounds right.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Forgetting that small っ, small ゃ ゅ ょ and the long vowel mark are separate beats. きて (kite, come) and きって (kitte, stamp) are different words, and おばさん (obasan, aunt) and おばあさん (obaasan, grandmother) are different people.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Mixing シ with ツ and ソ with ン. In シ and ン the two small strokes point down-ish and the long stroke rises; in ツ and ソ the small strokes point across and the long stroke falls. If a katakana word looks nonsense, you probably swapped one of these four.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Learning kanji as pictures with one sound attached. 日 is not \"hi\", it is \"day or sun\", and it can be read ひ, にち or じつ depending on the word. Learn 日本 and 日曜日 as whole words and the readings stop fighting you.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Three scripts, three jobs: hiragana for grammar, katakana for loanwords, kanji for meaning.</li>\n<li>46 basic hiragana and 46 basic katakana, both in the same gojūon vowel order a i u e o.</li>\n<li>Dakuten voices k s t h; handakuten turns h into p only.</li>\n<li>Small っ doubles a consonant, small ゃ ゅ ょ merge two kana into one sound, ー stretches a katakana vowel.</li>\n<li>Every kanji has a meaning, an on-yomi for compounds and a kun-yomi for standalone use.</li>\n<li>Radicals repeat: 氵 water, 亻 person, 木 tree, 口 mouth, 日 sun.</li>\n<li>Stroke order: top to bottom, left to right, horizontal before vertical, box last.</li>\n<li>Particles は, へ, を are written one way and pronounced another.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Write the romaji and meaning for these hiragana words: さくら, すし, ねこ, がっこう.", "a": "<p>Read each word mora by mora.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>さ (sa) + く (ku) + ら (ra) = さくら (sakura), cherry blossom.</li>\n<li>す (su) + し (shi) = すし (sushi), sushi.</li>\n<li>ね (ne) + こ (ko) = ねこ (neko), cat.</li>\n<li>が (ga) + っ (silent, doubles k) + こ (ko) + う (long u) = がっこう (gakkō), school.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The only tricky one is がっこう, because the small っ and the final う both add beats. Count four beats, not three.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "What does the dakuten do? Give the voiced form of か, さ, た and は, then the handakuten form of は.", "a": "<p>Dakuten (゛) is the two small strokes that voice an unvoiced consonant. It applies to the k, s, t and h rows.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Base</th><th>With dakuten</th><th>Handakuten</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>か (ka)</td><td>が (ga)</td><td>not used</td></tr>\n<tr><td>さ (sa)</td><td>ざ (za)</td><td>not used</td></tr>\n<tr><td>た (ta)</td><td>だ (da)</td><td>not used</td></tr>\n<tr><td>は (ha)</td><td>ば (ba)</td><td>ぱ (pa)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The handakuten (゜) is the small circle, and it exists only on the h-row, turning h into p. So は (ha), ば (ba), ぱ (pa) are three different kana from one base shape.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Read these katakana words and say what they mean: テレビ, パン, コーヒー, パソコン, カレー.", "a": "<p>Katakana words are almost always borrowed words, so say them fast and listen for the English or Portuguese original.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>テレビ (terebi), television, from \"television\" shortened.</li>\n<li>パン (pan), bread, borrowed from Portuguese.</li>\n<li>コーヒー (kōhī), coffee. The two ー marks make it four beats.</li>\n<li>パソコン (pasokon), a personal computer, from \"personal computer\" shortened.</li>\n<li>カレー (karē), curry. Again the ー stretches the final vowel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice the pattern: Japanese compresses long foreign words into the first few mora, and it never allows a consonant to end a word except ん.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Give the on-yomi and one kun-yomi for 山, 日 and 水, and use each kanji in one real word.", "a": "<p>On-yomi is the Chinese-derived reading, used in compounds. Kun-yomi is the native reading, used alone or with hiragana attached.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Kanji</th><th>On-yomi</th><th>Kun-yomi</th><th>Word</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>山</td><td>サン (san)</td><td>やま (yama)</td><td>富士山 (Fujisan) Mount Fuji; 山 (yama) mountain</td></tr>\n<tr><td>日</td><td>ニチ (nichi)</td><td>ひ (hi)</td><td>日本 (nihon) Japan; 日曜日 (nichiyōbi) Sunday</td></tr>\n<tr><td>水</td><td>スイ (sui)</td><td>みず (mizu)</td><td>水曜日 (suiyōbi) Wednesday; 水 (mizu) water</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The safe rule for N5: if the kanji is alone or followed by hiragana, expect kun-yomi. If two or more kanji sit together, expect on-yomi. 水曜日 shows both halves of the rule in one word, スイ plus ようび.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Read this sentence aloud and explain why each particle is spelled the way it is: わたし は がくせい です。Then write it using kanji where N5 allows.", "a": "<p>Reading: わたし は がくせい です (watashi wa gakusei desu), meaning \"I am a student\".</p>\n<p>The particle は is written with the kana for ha but pronounced wa, because it marks the topic. This is a historical spelling that never changed. The word がくせい (gakusei, student) is spelled が + く + せ + い, and the final い lengthens the せ, so it is four beats.</p>\n<p>です (desu) is the polite copula, the \"am, is, are\" of Japanese. The final す is devoiced in fast speech and sounds almost like \"des\", but you still write す.</p>\n<p>With kanji: 私は学生です。Here 私 (watashi) is I, 学生 (gakusei) is student. Both are on the N5 list. If you are unsure of a kanji under exam pressure, writing the whole thing in hiragana is not wrong, it is just slower to read.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>A common N5 item gives you a kana sentence and asks you to choose the version with correct kanji, or the reverse. Watch the particle は, because the wrong option often writes わ.</p></div>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "Each of these contains one error. Find it and fix it: (a) わたし わ がくせい です (b) おばあさん written as おばさん when you mean grandmother (c) きって written as きて when you mean postage stamp (d) コーヒ written without the long vowel marks.", "a": "<p>All four are spelling or length errors, not grammar errors.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) The topic particle is pronounced wa but written は. Correct: わたし は がくせい です。</li>\n<li>(b) Length changes meaning. おばさん (obasan) is aunt, おばあさん (obaasan) with the extra あ is grandmother. Add the second あ.</li>\n<li>(c) The small っ is a silent beat that doubles the next consonant. きって (kitte) is a stamp, きて (kite) is \"come\". Correct: きって.</li>\n<li>(d) コーヒー needs both ー marks, one after コ and one after ヒ. Without them the word is only two beats and reads as \"kohi\", which is not the borrowed word.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Under exam conditions these cost full marks, and they are the cheapest marks to protect: read your answer aloud in beats before you move on.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": "Semester_2/Japanese-ForeignLanguageProficiency/JP-M01.md"}, {"n": 2, "title": "Core Vocabulary", "tagline": "Numbers, time, family, places and verbs — the everyday words N5 questions are built from.", "minutes": 15, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Vocabulary is the part of N5 where effort pays back fastest. Grammar is a small closed system, but words are a long list, so the trick is to learn them in <em>clusters</em> instead of alphabetically. Numbers with counters. Time words with the particle that goes with them. Family words in two versions. Verbs by the group they conjugate in.</p>\n<p>Why clusters? Because N5 does not test words in isolation. It tests \"how much is this\", \"what time does the train leave\", \"who is this person\". If you have the number and the counter and the question word ready together, the sentence builds itself.</p>\n<p>A realistic target: around 800 words is the commonly cited vocabulary size for N5. That number is a rough community figure, not an official rule, so treat it as a direction of travel rather than a quota. Your syllabus simply says \"essential vocabulary for everyday communication\", and that is the standard to hold yourself to.</p>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>Three habits separate students who remember words from students who keep relearning them.</p>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">1</span><div><p>Learn every word with its part of speech. A noun can take を or が. A verb must be learnt with its dictionary form and its ます form together, because the group it belongs to decides every other form.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">2</span><div><p>Learn nouns with a particle glued on. Store がっこう に いきます (gakkō ni ikimasu, go to school), not just がっこう (gakkō, school). The particle is half the word.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">3</span><div><p>Say words out loud in beats. きょう (kyō, today) is two beats and きよう is not a word. Your ear catches mistakes your eyes miss.</p></div></div></div>\n<p>Remember also that N5 tests the polite register. Learn たべます (tabemasu, eat) as your default, and add the plain たべる (taberu) as the dictionary form you need for looking things up.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.1", "title": "Numbers and the counter system", "html": "<p>The numbers 1 to 10 are the foundation: いち (ichi), に (ni), さん (san), よん (yon, also し shi), ご (go), ろく (roku), なな (nana, also しち shichi), はち (hachi), きゅう (kyū, also く ku), じゅう (jū).</p>\n<p>Japanese has no separate plural form. What it has instead is a <strong>counter</strong> (助数詞, josūshi), a suffix that changes with the shape or kind of thing being counted. \"Two\" is not enough, you must say two of what.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Counter</th><th>Used for</th><th>Two of them</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>〜つ (-tsu)</td><td>generic things, 1 to 10 only</td><td>ふたつ (futatsu)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>〜人 (-nin)</td><td>people</td><td>ふたり (futari)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>〜本 (-hon)</td><td>long thin objects, bottles</td><td>にほん (nihon)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>〜枚 (-mai)</td><td>flat things, paper, shirts</td><td>にまい (nimai)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>〜台 (-dai)</td><td>machines, vehicles</td><td>にだい (nidai)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>〜匹 (-hiki)</td><td>small animals</td><td>にひき (nihiki)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>〜冊 (-satsu)</td><td>books</td><td>にさつ (nisatsu)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>〜歳 (-sai)</td><td>years of age</td><td>にさい (nisai)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>〜杯 (-hai)</td><td>cups, glasses</td><td>にはい (nihai)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>〜回 (-kai)</td><td>times, occurrences</td><td>にかい (nikai)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two things to notice. First, 〜つ is irregular: ひとつ (hitotsu), ふたつ (futatsu), みっつ (mittsu), よっつ (yottsu), いつつ (itsutsu), むっつ (muttsu), ななつ (nanatsu), やっつ (yattsu), ここのつ (kokonotsu), とお (tō). Second, people 1 and 2 are special: ひとり (hitori) and ふたり (futari), not いちにん and ににん.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.2", "title": "Telling time and dates", "html": "<p>Hours are built with 〜時 (-ji): いちじ (ichiji), にじ (niji), さんじ (sanji), よじ (yoji), ごじ (goji), ろくじ (rokuji), しちじ (shichiji), はちじ (hachiji), くじ (kuji), じゅうじ (jūji). Note よじ not よんじ, and くじ not きゅうじ. Half past is 〜じはん (-ji han), so さんじはん (sanji han) is 3:30.</p>\n<p>Minutes use 〜分 (-fun), and the sound shifts with the number: いっぷん (ippun), にふん (nifun), さんぷん (sanpun), よんぷん (yonpun), ごふん (gofun), ろっぷん (roppun), ななふん (nanafun), はっぷん (happun), きゅうふん (kyūfun), じゅっぷん (juppun).</p>\n<p>Dates are harder than hours because the first ten days have their own names.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Reading</th><th>Day</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1st</td><td>ついたち (tsuitachi)</td><td>6th</td><td>むいか (muika)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2nd</td><td>ふつか (futsuka)</td><td>7th</td><td>なのか (nanoka)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3rd</td><td>みっか (mikka)</td><td>8th</td><td>ようか (yōka)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4th</td><td>よっか (yokka)</td><td>9th</td><td>ここのか (kokonoka)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5th</td><td>いつか (itsuka)</td><td>10th</td><td>とおか (tōka)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>From the 11th it becomes regular 〜にち (-nichi), except the 14th じゅうよっか (jūyokka), the 20th はつか (hatsuka) and the 24th にじゅうよっか (nijūyokka). Months are simply 〜がつ (-gatsu): いちがつ, にがつ, さんがつ, しがつ, ごがつ, ろくがつ, しちがつ, はちがつ, くがつ, じゅうがつ, じゅういちがつ, じゅうにがつ. Watch しがつ (April) and しちがつ (July).</p>\n<p>Weekdays follow the sun, moon and five visible planets: げつようび (getsuyōbi, Monday), かようび (kayōbi), すいようび (suiyōbi), もくようび (mokuyōbi), きんようび (kin'yōbi), どようび (doyōbi), にちようび (nichiyōbi, Sunday).</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.3", "title": "People, family and the two versions rule", "html": "<p>Family words come in pairs, and picking the wrong one is a politeness error, not a vocabulary error. When you talk about <em>your own</em> family to someone else, you use the humble set. When you talk about <em>someone else's</em> family, you use the respectful set, usually with お〜.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Relation</th><th>My family (humble)</th><th>Your family (respectful)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>father</td><td>ちち (chichi)</td><td>おとうさん (otōsan)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>mother</td><td>はは (haha)</td><td>おかあさん (okāsan)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>elder brother</td><td>あに (ani)</td><td>おにいさん (oniisan)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>elder sister</td><td>あね (ane)</td><td>おねえさん (onēsan)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>younger brother</td><td>おとうと (otōto)</td><td>おとうとさん (otōtosan)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>younger sister</td><td>いもうと (imōto)</td><td>いもうとさん (imōtosan)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>child</td><td>こども (kodomo)</td><td>おこさん (okosan)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>grandparent</td><td>そふ / そぼ (sofu / sobo)</td><td>おじいさん / おばあさん (ojiisan / obāsan)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>So \"this is my father\" is これは ちち です (kore wa chichi desu), but \"is your father well?\" is おとうさん は おげんき ですか (otōsan wa ogenki desu ka). Calling your own father おとうさん to an outsider sounds like you are praising him, which is why the humble form exists.</p>\n<p>The group words are worth learning as a block: かぞく (kazoku, family), りょうしん (ryōshin, parents), きょうだい (kyōdai, siblings), ともだち (tomodachi, friend), せんせい (sensei, teacher), がくせい (gakusei, student), しゃいん (shain, company employee).</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.4", "title": "Places, things and getting around", "html": "<p>Place nouns are the backbone of N5 listening questions, because almost every dialogue involves going somewhere. Learn them with the particle に or へ for destination and で for the place where an action happens.</p>\n<div class=\"grid2\"><div><h4>Places</h4><p>がっこう (gakkō) school, ぎんこう (ginkō) bank, ゆうびんきょく (yūbinkyoku) post office, びょういん (byōin) hospital, えき (eki) station, くうこう (kūkō) airport, みせ (mise) shop, としょかん (toshokan) library, こうえん (kōen) park, レストラン (resutoran) restaurant, スーパー (sūpā) supermarket, うち (uchi) home.</p></div><div><h4>Transport</h4><p>でんしゃ (densha) train, バス (basu) bus, くるま (kuruma) car, じてんしゃ (jitensha) bicycle, ひこうき (hikōki) aeroplane, タクシー (takushī) taxi. On foot is あるいて (aruite), as in あるいて いきます (aruite ikimasu, go on foot).</p></div></div>\n<p>Everyday objects you will meet in reading passages: ほん (hon) book, ペン (pen) pen, かさ (kasa) umbrella, とけい (tokei) watch or clock, かばん (kaban) bag, でんわ (denwa) telephone, しんぶん (shinbun) newspaper, しゃしん (shashin) photograph, おかね (okane) money, みず (mizu) water, ごはん (gohan) rice or a meal, パン (pan) bread, おちゃ (ocha) tea, コーヒー (kōhī) coffee.</p>\n<p>Notice that transport takes the particle で for \"by means of\": でんしゃ で いきます (densha de ikimasu, go by train). Same で as \"place of action\", which is confusing at first but convenient later, because both answer \"how or where\".</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "2.5", "title": "Verbs, question words and frequency", "html": "<p>Learn verbs in pairs: the polite ます form for speaking and the dictionary form for identification. Both are given below, because the dictionary form is what tells you the verb group.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>ます form</th><th>Dictionary form</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>いきます (ikimasu)</td><td>行く (iku)</td><td>go</td></tr>\n<tr><td>きます (kimasu)</td><td>来る (kuru)</td><td>come</td></tr>\n<tr><td>かえります (kaerimasu)</td><td>帰る (kaeru)</td><td>return home</td></tr>\n<tr><td>たべます (tabemasu)</td><td>食べる (taberu)</td><td>eat</td></tr>\n<tr><td>のみます (nomimasu)</td><td>飲む (nomu)</td><td>drink</td></tr>\n<tr><td>みます (mimasu)</td><td>見る (miru)</td><td>see, watch</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ききます (kikimasu)</td><td>聞く (kiku)</td><td>listen, ask</td></tr>\n<tr><td>はなします (hanashimasu)</td><td>話す (hanasu)</td><td>speak</td></tr>\n<tr><td>よみます (yomimasu)</td><td>読む (yomu)</td><td>read</td></tr>\n<tr><td>かきます (kakimasu)</td><td>書く (kaku)</td><td>write</td></tr>\n<tr><td>かいます (kaimasu)</td><td>買う (kau)</td><td>buy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>まちます (machimasu)</td><td>待つ (matsu)</td><td>wait</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ねます (nemasu)</td><td>寝る (neru)</td><td>sleep</td></tr>\n<tr><td>おきます (okimasu)</td><td>起きる (okiru)</td><td>get up</td></tr>\n<tr><td>べんきょうします (benkyō shimasu)</td><td>勉強する (benkyō suru)</td><td>study</td></tr>\n<tr><td>わかります (wakarimasu)</td><td>分かる (wakaru)</td><td>understand</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Question words replace the answer, they do not move to the front. あなた は どこ に いきます か (anata wa doko ni ikimasu ka) is \"where are you going\", with どこ (doko, where) sitting exactly where the place name would sit.</p>\n<p>The set to own: なに (nani, what), だれ (dare, who), どこ (doko, where), いつ (itsu, when), どうして (dōshite, why), どう (dō, how), いくら (ikura, how much), いくつ (ikutsu, how many or how old), どれ (dore, which one), なんじ (nanji, what time), なんにん (nannin, how many people).</p>\n<p>Finally, frequency adverbs slot in before the verb: いつも (itsumo, always), よく (yoku, often), ときどき (tokidoki, sometimes), あまり (amari, not much, needs a negative verb), ぜんぜん (zenzen, not at all, needs a negative verb). あまり たべません (amari tabemasen) is \"I do not eat much\".</p>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Rules that generate new words", "html": "<p>You do not memorise every number. You memorise the rules that build them.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">11 = 10 + 1 → じゅういち (jūichi)</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Numbers 11 to 19 are じゅう plus the unit digit, in that order.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Any number above ten. Above ten the unit goes after, never before.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> じゅうご (jūgo) 15, じゅうはち (jūhachi) 18.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">20 = 2 × 10 → にじゅう (nijū)</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Tens are digit plus じゅう, and hundreds are digit plus ひゃく.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> 20, 30, 40 and above. Combine both rules for 35 = さんじゅうご.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> よんじゅうに (yonjūni) 42, ひゃく (hyaku) 100, にひゃく (nihyaku) 200.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">irregular sound changes: 300 さんびゃく, 600 ろっぴゃく, 800 はっぴゃく</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Three hundred, six hundred and eight hundred voice or double the h.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Same idea for thousands: 3000 さんぜん, 8000 はっせん. Ten thousand is いちまん (ichiman).</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> ろっぴゃくえん (roppyaku en) 600 yen, はっせんルピー (hassen rupī) 8,000 rupees.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">counter sound changes follow the number 1, 3, 6, 8, 10</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Those five numbers trigger doubling or voicing in most counters.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> 本, 匹, 杯, 冊, 歳 all behave this way. Learn one counter properly and the rest are predictable.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> いっぽん (ippon) 1 bottle, さんぼん (sanbon) 3 bottles, ろっぽん (roppon) 6, はっぽん (happon) 8, じゅっぽん (juppon) 10.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why counters exist</span><p>English does this too and you never noticed: \"two sheets of paper\", \"three head of cattle\", \"four loaves of bread\". Japanese simply makes the counter compulsory and puts it directly after the number.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — telling the time</h4>\n<p>Say 4:30 and 7:15. Hours: よじ (yoji), しちじ (shichiji). Minutes: さんじゅっぷん (sanjuppun) 30, じゅうごふん (jūgofun) 15. Or use はん for half.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">よじはん (yoji han) 4:30 · しちじ じゅうごふん (shichiji jūgofun) 7:15</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — counting three different things</h4>\n<p>Three bottles, three sheets of paper, three people. Different counter each time, and 3 is one of the numbers that shifts the sound.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">さんぼん (sanbon) 3 bottles · さんまい (sanmai) 3 sheets · さんにん (sannin) 3 people</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 3 — a shop dialogue</h4>\n<p>すみません、この かさ は いくら ですか (sumimasen, kono kasa wa ikura desu ka) — excuse me, how much is this umbrella? Answer: せんえん です (sen en desu), it is 1,000 yen. Then これを ください (kore o kudasai), please give me this one.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">いくら (ikura) how much · せんえん (sen en) 1,000 yen</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 4 — frequency in a real sentence</h4>\n<p>わたし は あまり コーヒー を のみません (watashi wa amari kōhī o nomimasen). あまり must sit with a negative verb, so のみません, not のみます.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">I do not drink much coffee.</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Using あまり or ぜんぜん with a positive verb. Both demand a negative: ぜんぜん わかりません (zenzen wakarimasen), I do not understand at all. ぜんぜん わかります is wrong.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Mixing the humble and respectful family sets. Talking about your own father as おとうさん to a stranger is a politeness mistake even though the word is real. Own family: ちち, はは. Their family: おとうさん, おかあさん.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Reading 四時 as よんじ and 九時 as きゅうじ. The correct readings are よじ (yoji) and くじ (kuji). Similarly 四月 is しがつ (shigatsu) and 七月 is しちがつ (shichigatsu).</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Forgetting the counter entirely. にほん can mean \"two bottles\" or \"Japan\" depending on context, and ふたり is two people while ににん is simply wrong. If a number stands alone, you probably dropped a counter.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>N5 listening and reading love numbers: a price, a time, a date, a count. The distractors are always near-misses like しちじ against いちじ, or よっか against ようか. Write the number down as digits the moment you hear it.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Numbers build by addition then multiplication: 35 = さんじゅうご.</li>\n<li>Irregulars to drill: さんびゃく, ろっぴゃく, はっぴゃく, さんぜん, はっせん.</li>\n<li>Counters by shape: 〜本 long, 〜枚 flat, 〜台 machines, 〜匹 small animals, 〜人 people.</li>\n<li>People 1 and 2 are ひとり and ふたり, never いちにん or ににん.</li>\n<li>Hours to watch: よじ 4, しちじ 7, くじ 9. First ten dates are irregular.</li>\n<li>Own family humble (ちち, はは), other family respectful (おとうさん, おかあさん).</li>\n<li>あまり and ぜんぜん need a negative verb.</li>\n<li>Question word stays where the answer would go.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Write in kana: 15, 42, 300, 8,000.", "a": "<p>Apply the two building rules: tens are digit plus じゅう, hundreds are digit plus ひゃく.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>15 = 10 + 5 = じゅうご (jūgo).</li>\n<li>42 = 4 × 10 + 2 = よんじゅうに (yonjūni).</li>\n<li>300 = 3 × 100, and 3 is an irregular, so さんびゃく (sanbyaku), not さんひゃく.</li>\n<li>8,000 = 8 × 1000, also irregular: はっせん (hassen), not はちせん.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The trap is that only 3, 6 and 8 misbehave in the hundreds, and only 3 and 8 in the thousands, so 200 is plain にひゃく (nihyaku) and 600 is ろっぴゃく (roppyaku).</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Count these correctly: 2 people, 4 books, 6 bottles, 3 flat shirts, 10 small animals.", "a": "<p>Pick the counter first, then apply the sound change if the number is 1, 3, 6, 8 or 10.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Counter</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>2 people</td><td>〜人</td><td>ふたり (futari), the irregular pair form</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4 books</td><td>〜冊</td><td>よんさつ (yonsatsu)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6 bottles</td><td>〜本</td><td>ろっぽん (roppon), 6 doubles the p</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3 flat shirts</td><td>〜枚</td><td>さんまい (sanmai), no change with 枚</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10 small animals</td><td>〜匹</td><td>じゅっぴき (juppiki)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Note that 〜枚 never changes sound, while 〜本, 〜匹 and 〜杯 do. Two people is the one to memorise cold, because ににん is a classic wrong answer.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Say these times and dates in Japanese: 9:30, 4:15, the 3rd of the month, the 20th of the month, Wednesday.", "a": "<p>Hours use 〜時, minutes use 〜分, and half past is はん.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>9:30 — くじはん (kuji han). Note くじ, not きゅうじ.</li>\n<li>4:15 — よじ じゅうごふん (yoji jūgofun). Note よじ, not よんじ.</li>\n<li>The 3rd — みっか (mikka). The first ten days are irregular names, not さんにち.</li>\n<li>The 20th — はつか (hatsuka), a one-off irregular like the 14th じゅうよっか and the 24th にじゅうよっか.</li>\n<li>Wednesday — すいようび (suiyōbi), literally water day, 水 (sui) plus ようび.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For the date you then add に if it is a time of an action: みっか に いきます (mikka ni ikimasu), I will go on the 3rd.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "You are talking to your teacher. Say correctly: (a) this is my mother (b) is your elder brother well (c) I have two younger sisters.", "a": "<p>The rule: humble set for my family, respectful set for theirs.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) これは はは です (kore wa haha desu). Using おかあさん here would be praising your own mother to an outsider, which is the error being tested.</li>\n<li>(b) おにいさん は おげんき ですか (oniisan wa ogenki desu ka). おにいさん is the respectful form, correct for the teacher's family.</li>\n<li>(c) いもうと が ふたり います (imōto ga futari imasu). Sisters are my family, so humble いもうと. Two people is ふたり, and existence uses います for living things with the particle が.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note that (c) uses が rather than は, because the sisters are the new information being introduced.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Build sentences: (a) I go to school by bicycle (b) I study Japanese every morning (c) I do not drink much tea (d) how many people are in your family?", "a": "<p>Watch the particles: に or へ for destination, で for means, を for the object of a transitive verb.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) わたし は じてんしゃ で がっこう に いきます (watashi wa jitensha de gakkō ni ikimasu). で marks the means of transport, に the destination.</li>\n<li>(b) わたし は まいあさ にほんご を べんきょうします (watashi wa maiasa nihongo o benkyō shimasu). まいあさ is every morning, を marks the thing studied.</li>\n<li>(c) わたし は あまり おちゃ を のみません (watashi wa amari ocha o nomimasen). あまり forces the negative のみません.</li>\n<li>(d) かぞく は なんにん ですか (kazoku wa nannin desu ka). なんにん is the people-counter question word, and it sits where the number would sit.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If you got (c) with のみます, re-read the frequency rule: あまり and ぜんぜん always pair with a negative.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "A passage says: わたし は まいにち くじ から ごじ まで がっこう で べんきょうします。ろくじ に うち に かえります。それから よる は あまり テレビ を みません。Summarise the day in English and name every particle.", "a": "<p>Translation: every day I study at school from nine o'clock until five, go home at six, and after that I do not watch much television at night.</p>\n<p>Particles, one by one:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Particle</th><th>Job here</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>は (wa)</td><td>marks わたし and よる as topic</td></tr>\n<tr><td>から</td><td>from, the starting point of the time span</td></tr>\n<tr><td>まで</td><td>until, the end point</td></tr>\n<tr><td>で</td><td>place where the action happens, at school</td></tr>\n<tr><td>に</td><td>point in time, at six</td></tr>\n<tr><td>を</td><td>object of みません, the television</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two things a careful reader notices. くじ から ごじ まで is a span, so both から and まで appear. And あまり テレビ を みません is negative, so the meaning is \"not much\", not \"never\". A careless reader turns that into \"I never watch TV\", which is a different sentence and the usual distractor in N5 reading items.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": "Semester_2/Japanese-ForeignLanguageProficiency/JP-M02.md"}, {"n": 3, "title": "Core Grammar — Sentence Patterns, Verbs, Adjectives, Particles", "tagline": "Build correct polite sentences: noun sentences, the three verb groups, both adjective types and the particles.", "minutes": 18, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Japanese grammar is not hard, it is <em>front-loaded</em>. English puts the verb near the front and hangs the rest off it. Japanese puts the verb at the very end and marks every other job with a small particle. So the sentence arrives in one direction and you cannot guess its meaning until the last word lands.</p>\n<p>Everything in N5 sits on four supports: noun sentences with です (desu), the three verb groups with ます (masu), the two adjective types, and the particles that glue them together. Learn those four and you can say most of what a first-year student needs to say.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>N5 tests the polite register, meaning です and ます forms. Plain forms exist and you will see them inside quoted speech, but when you write an answer, produce です and ます. Keep that as your default and you will not lose marks for register.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>Three ideas explain almost every sentence you will build.</p>\n<p><strong>Word order is subject-object-verb.</strong> わたし は ごはん を たべます (watashi wa gohan o tabemasu) is literally \"I rice eat\". The verb is always last. Nothing moves it, not even a question.</p>\n<p><strong>Particles mark jobs, not positions.</strong> は marks the topic, が the subject, を the object, に the destination or time, で the place or means. Because the job is stamped on the word, Japanese can reorder things freely and still be understood. That is why the same sentence can start with the time: あした わたし は ごはん を たべます.</p>\n<p><strong>There is no plural and no gender.</strong> がくせい (gakusei) covers one student or ten. たべます covers \"I eat\", \"you eat\", \"we eat\". Context, or an explicit topic, decides. This feels loose at first and then becomes a relief.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why the verb goes last</span><p>In Japanese the verb carries the tense and the politeness, and it also decides what the sentence can contain. Putting it last lets the listener hold everything in memory and then close the sentence. It also means you should never translate word by word from English, build from the verb backwards instead.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.1", "title": "Noun sentences: the です frame", "html": "<p>The simplest complete sentence is \"A is B\", and Japanese builds it as A は B です. Here は (wa) is the topic particle and です (desu) is the polite copula, roughly \"am, is, are\".</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">わたし は がくせい です。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> watashi wa gakusei desu, \"I am a student\". わたし (watashi) is I, がくせい (gakusei) is student.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Any time you identify or describe something with a noun: name, job, nationality, identity.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> これは ほん です (kore wa hon desu), this is a book.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>The frame has three switches, and all of them sit on です.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Meaning</th><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>is</td><td>です (desu)</td><td>がくせい です (gakusei desu) is a student</td></tr>\n<tr><td>is not</td><td>じゃありません (ja arimasen)</td><td>がくせい じゃありません (gakusei ja arimasen)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>was</td><td>でした (deshita)</td><td>がくせい でした (gakusei deshita) was a student</td></tr>\n<tr><td>was not</td><td>じゃありませんでした (ja arimasen deshita)</td><td>がくせい じゃありませんでした</td></tr>\n<tr><td>question</td><td>ですか (desu ka)</td><td>がくせい ですか (gakusei desu ka) are you a student?</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two more pieces make this frame useful. の (no) links two nouns, and it works in both directions: わたし の ほん (watashi no hon) is my book, and にほんご の せんせい (nihongo no sensei) is a teacher of Japanese. も (mo) replaces は to mean \"also\": わたし も がくせい です (watashi mo gakusei desu), I am a student too.</p>\n<p>One caution that trips up every beginner: です does not mean \"to be\" in the sense of existing. \"There is a book\" is not ほん です. Existence needs あります or います, which come later in this module.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.2", "title": "Pointing words: これ, この and friends", "html": "<p>Japanese has a neat three-way pointer system. The three distances are: near me, near you, and far from both. Learn it as a grid and you get twelve words for the price of four.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>near me</th><th>near you</th><th>far from both</th><th>question</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>thing alone</td><td>これ (kore)</td><td>それ (sore)</td><td>あれ (are)</td><td>どれ (dore)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>thing + noun</td><td>この (kono)</td><td>その (sono)</td><td>あの (ano)</td><td>どの (dono)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>place</td><td>ここ (koko)</td><td>そこ (soko)</td><td>あそこ (asoko)</td><td>どこ (doko)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>direction</td><td>こちら (kochira)</td><td>そちら (sochira)</td><td>あちら (achira)</td><td>どちら (dochira)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The pattern is mechanical: こ for near me, そ for near you, あ for far, ど for the question. Once you see that, ここ and どこ stop being separate vocabulary items.</p>\n<p>Use これ alone, and この with a noun. これは ほん です (kore wa hon desu) is \"this is a book\". この ほん は わたし の です (kono hon wa watashi no desu) is \"this book is mine\". Saying これ ほん です is the classic error, because これ already contains \"thing\" and cannot take another noun after it.</p>\n<p>どちら is also the polite way to ask \"which one\" when the choice is between two things, and it doubles as \"where\" in polite speech: おくには は どちら ですか (okuni wa dochira desu ka), which country are you from?</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.3", "title": "Verbs: the three groups and the ます grid", "html": "<p>Japanese verbs come in three groups, and the group decides every form you will ever make. Identify the group from the dictionary form, the plain form you find in a dictionary, which always ends in a u-sound.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>How to spot it</th><th>Examples</th><th>ます form</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Group 1 (godan, u-verbs)</td><td>dictionary form ends in a u-sound that is not -eru or -iru</td><td>行く (iku) go, 飲む (nomu) drink, 話す (hanasu) speak</td><td>change final u to i: 行きます, 飲みます, 話します</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Group 2 (ichidan, ru-verbs)</td><td>ends in -eru or -iru</td><td>食べる (taberu) eat, 見る (miru) see, 寝る (neru) sleep</td><td>drop る, add ます: 食べます, 見ます, 寝ます</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Group 3 (irregular)</td><td>only two verbs</td><td>する (suru) do, 来る (kuru) come</td><td>します (shimasu), きます (kimasu)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Group 2 looks easy and then betrays you. 帰る (kaeru) ends in -eru but is Group 1, so it is 帰ります (kaerimasu). The same is true of 入る (hairu, enter) and 走る (hashiru, run). There are only a handful of these exceptions and they are all common, so memorise them as a short list.</p>\n<p>Once you have ます, the whole polite grid is four forms and no exceptions.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Affirmative</th><th>Negative</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Present / future</td><td>たべます (tabemasu)</td><td>たべません (tabemasen)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Past</td><td>たべました (tabemashita)</td><td>たべませんでした (tabemasen deshita)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Note that present and future are the same form. たべます means \"I eat\" and \"I will eat\", and only a time word such as あした (ashita, tomorrow) or まいにち (mainichi, every day) tells you which.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.4", "title": "The two adjective families", "html": "<p>Japanese adjectives are verbs in disguise: they carry tense and negation themselves. There are exactly two families, and mixing them up is the most common grammar error in N5 writing.</p>\n<p><strong>い-adjectives</strong> end in い in the dictionary form. たかい (takai) is expensive or tall. To conjugate, you chop nothing, you change the い.</p>\n<p><strong>な-adjectives</strong> do not end in い in their dictionary form, and they need な before a noun. しずか (shizuka) is quiet, and しずかな へや (shizuka na heya) is a quiet room.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>い-adjective: たかい (takai)</th><th>な-adjective: しずか (shizuka)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>present affirmative</td><td>たかいです</td><td>しずかです</td></tr>\n<tr><td>present negative</td><td>たかくないです</td><td>しずかじゃないです</td></tr>\n<tr><td>past affirmative</td><td>たかかったです</td><td>しずかでした</td></tr>\n<tr><td>past negative</td><td>たかくなかったです</td><td>しずかじゃなかったです</td></tr>\n<tr><td>before a noun</td><td>たかい やま (takai yama) a tall mountain</td><td>しずかな まち (shizuka na machi) a quiet town</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Look at what happens: the い-adjective changes shape and keeps です as a plain politeness marker. The な-adjective never changes shape at all, it borrows the です grid, which is why しずかでした is correct and しずかかった is nonsense.</p>\n<p>One adjective is fully irregular and very common. いい (ii, good) conjugates as if it were よい: よくないです (not good), よかったです (was good), よくなかったです (was not good).</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>きれい (kirei, beautiful or clean) and ゆうめい (yūmei, famous) end in an i-sound but are な-adjectives. So it is きれいな はな (kirei na hana), a beautiful flower, and never きれいかった. Judge by the family the word belongs to, not by the last sound.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.5", "title": "Particles: who does what to whom", "html": "<p>Particles are one-beat words that stick to the end of a noun and label its job. There are about ten you need, and they do all the work English does with word order and prepositions.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Particle</th><th>Job</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>は (wa)</td><td>topic, \"as for ...\"</td><td>わたし は (watashi wa) as for me</td></tr>\n<tr><td>が (ga)</td><td>subject, especially new information</td><td>だれ が きました か (dare ga kimashita ka) who came?</td></tr>\n<tr><td>を (o)</td><td>direct object of a transitive verb</td><td>パン を たべます (pan o tabemasu) eat bread</td></tr>\n<tr><td>に (ni)</td><td>destination, point in time, recipient</td><td>がっこう に いきます (gakkō ni ikimasu) go to school</td></tr>\n<tr><td>で (de)</td><td>place of action, means or tool</td><td>がっこう で べんきょうします (gakkō de benkyō shimasu) study at school</td></tr>\n<tr><td>へ (e)</td><td>direction of movement, same as に here</td><td>にほん へ いきます (nihon e ikimasu) go to Japan</td></tr>\n<tr><td>と (to)</td><td>and (complete list), with (a person)</td><td>ともだち と いきます (tomodachi to ikimasu) go with a friend</td></tr>\n<tr><td>も (mo)</td><td>also, too, replaces は and が</td><td>わたし も いきます (watashi mo ikimasu) I will go too</td></tr>\n<tr><td>の (no)</td><td>possession, belonging, description</td><td>せんせい の ほん (sensei no hon) the teacher's book</td></tr>\n<tr><td>から / まで</td><td>from / until</td><td>くじ から ごじ まで (kuji kara goji made) from 9 to 5</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The は versus が choice is the hardest thing in this module and N5 only tests the easy half of it. Use は when the thing is already known or is the frame of the sentence. Use が when the thing is the answer to a question, or is being introduced for the first time. だれ が きました か (dare ga kimashita ka) asks who came, so the answer takes が: たなかさん が きました (Tanaka-san ga kimashita), Tanaka came.</p>\n<p>で versus に is the other pair worth drilling. に marks where something ends up, で marks where an action happens. がっこう に いきます is going to school, but がっこう で べんきょうします is studying at school. Same place, different particle, because one is movement and the other is activity.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "3.6", "title": "Existence: あります and います", "html": "<p>English has one \"there is\". Japanese has two, and the choice is not optional: あります (arimasu) for things that do not move on their own, います (imasu) for living creatures.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">つくえ の うえ に ほん が あります。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> tsukue no ue ni hon ga arimasu, \"there is a book on the desk\". つくえ (tsukue) desk, うえ (ue) top, ほん (hon) book.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Stating that something exists, or where it exists. The place takes に, the thing takes が.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> れいぞうこ に ビール が あります (reizōko ni bīru ga arimasu), there is beer in the fridge.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>The pattern is fixed and worth memorising as a shape: <span class=\"chip\">place に thing が あります / います</span>. The thing takes が, not は, because it is the new information. The place takes に, because existence is a kind of location.</p>\n<p>For people and animals: きょうしつ に がくせい が います (kyōshitsu ni gakusei ga imasu), there are students in the classroom. A teacher is います, a chair is あります. Plants and trees are usually あります because they do not walk.</p>\n<p>Flip the order and the topic changes, which is a useful trick. ほん は つくえ の うえ に あります (hon wa tsukue no ue ni arimasu) means \"the book is on the desk\", with ほん as the topic. Both sentences are correct, the difference is what you are talking about, the book or the desk.</p>\n<p>Negation and past work as usual: ありません (arimasen) there is not, いました (imashita) there was. So きのう は だれ も いませんでした (kinō wa dare mo imasen deshita), yesterday nobody was there.</p>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Conjugation grids and rules", "html": "<p>Keep these four grids in one place in your notebook. Everything else in N5 grammar is built on them.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">Group 1: final u → i, then add ます</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The dictionary form's last syllable moves to the i-row before ます attaches.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Any verb whose dictionary form is not -eru or -iru, plus the exceptions 帰る, 入る, 走る.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> 書く (kaku) → 書きます (kakimasu), 買う (kau) → 買います (kaimasu), 待つ (matsu) → 待ちます (machimasu).</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">Group 2: drop る, then add ます</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Nothing is left but the stem, and ます goes straight on.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Verbs ending in -eru or -iru, except the three Group 1 verbs above.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> 食べる (taberu) → 食べます (tabemasu), 起きる (okiru) → 起きます (okimasu).</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">い-adjective: い → くない / かった / くなかった</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The final い is the moving part. Replace it and keep です for politeness.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Every い-adjective except いい, which behaves as よい.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> あつい (atsui, hot) → あつくないです, あつかったです, あつくなかったです.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">な-adjective: no change, borrow the です grid</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The word itself never changes. Only です changes, and な appears before a noun.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> All な-adjectives, including きれい and ゆうめい which end in an i-sound.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> しずか (shizuka, quiet) → しずかじゃないです, しずかでした, しずかな へや.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Verb group</th><th>Dictionary form</th><th>ます</th><th>ません</th><th>ました</th><th>ませんでした</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>行く (iku)</td><td>行きます</td><td>行きません</td><td>行きました</td><td>行きませんでした</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>帰る (kaeru)</td><td>帰ります</td><td>帰りません</td><td>帰りました</td><td>帰りませんでした</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>見る (miru)</td><td>見ます</td><td>見ません</td><td>見ました</td><td>見ませんでした</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>する (suru)</td><td>します</td><td>しません</td><td>しました</td><td>しませんでした</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>来る (kuru)</td><td>きます</td><td>きません</td><td>きました</td><td>きませんでした</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — a noun sentence, all four switches</h4>\n<p>Topic わたし, noun インドじん (Indo-jin, Indian). Present: わたし は インドじん です。Negative: わたし は インドじん じゃありません。Question: あなた は インドじん ですか。</p>\n<div class=\"res\">わたし は インドじん です (watashi wa Indo-jin desu) — I am Indian.</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — conjugating a Group 1 verb</h4>\n<p>のむ (nomu, drink) is Group 1 because it ends in -mu. Move the final syllable to the i-row: のみ. Then add the four endings.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">のみます / のみません / のみました / のみませんでした</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 3 — adjective agreement with a noun</h4>\n<p>\"A quiet, expensive restaurant.\" しずか is a な-adjective so it needs な: しずかな. たかい is an い-adjective so it stays bare: たかい. レストラン (resutoran) is restaurant.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">しずかで たかい レストラン (shizuka de takai resutoran)</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 4 — あります versus います</h4>\n<p>\"There is a cat in the garden\" and \"there is a table in the garden\". The cat is alive, so います. The table is not, so あります. Both put the place first with に and the thing with が.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">にわ に ねこ が います (niwa ni neko ga imasu) · にわ に テーブル が あります (niwa ni tēburu ga arimasu)</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Adding な to an い-adjective or changing a な-adjective like an い one. Correct: たかい ほん (takai hon), しずかな へや (shizuka na heya). Wrong: たかいな ほん, しずかかった.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Putting the verb somewhere other than the end. わたし は たべます ごはん を is broken Japanese even though every word is right. The verb closes the sentence, always.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Treating 帰る (kaeru) as a Group 2 verb because it ends in -eru. It is Group 1: 帰ります (kaerimasu), not 帰ます. Same for 入る (hairu) and 走る (hashiru).</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Using あります for people. がくせい が あります says students are inanimate objects. Use います (imasu) for anything that moves by itself.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Attaching です directly to a verb. たべます です is wrong. Verbs already carry politeness in ます, so です is unnecessary. です belongs with nouns and adjectives only.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Word order is subject-object-verb, and the verb always comes last.</li>\n<li>Noun sentence: A は B です, negative じゃありません, past でした, question ですか.</li>\n<li>Three verb groups: Group 1 final u to i, Group 2 drop る, Group 3 する and 来る.</li>\n<li>Polite grid: ます / ません / ました / ませんでした. Present also covers future.</li>\n<li>い-adjectives change the い; な-adjectives never change and take な before a noun.</li>\n<li>いい is irregular: よくない, よかった, よくなかった.</li>\n<li>Particles: は topic, が subject, を object, に destination or time, で place or means.</li>\n<li>あります for things, います for living creatures, in the shape place に thing が.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Classify these verbs into groups 1, 2 and 3, then give the ます form: 飲む (nomu), 食べる (taberu), 来る (kuru), 帰る (kaeru), 書く (kaku), 見る (miru).", "a": "<p>Identify by the dictionary form first, then conjugate by the group rule.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>Group</th><th>ます form</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>飲む (nomu) drink</td><td>1, ends -mu</td><td>飲みます (nomimasu)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>食べる (taberu) eat</td><td>2, ends -eru</td><td>食べます (tabemasu)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>来る (kuru) come</td><td>3, irregular</td><td>きます (kimasu)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>帰る (kaeru) return</td><td>1, exception despite -eru</td><td>帰ります (kaerimasu)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>書く (kaku) write</td><td>1, ends -ku</td><td>書きます (kakimasu)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>見る (miru) see</td><td>2, ends -iru</td><td>見ます (mimasu)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>帰る is the item to study here. Three common verbs break the -eru / -iru rule: 帰る, 入る (hairu, enter) and 走る (hashiru, run). All three are Group 1.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Give all four polite forms (present, present negative, past, past negative) of たかい (takai) and しずか (shizuka).", "a": "<p>たかい is an い-adjective, so the final い is what moves. しずか is a な-adjective, so it does not move at all and です does the work.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>たかい (takai)</th><th>しずか (shizuka)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>present</td><td>たかいです</td><td>しずかです</td></tr>\n<tr><td>present negative</td><td>たかくないです</td><td>しずかじゃないです</td></tr>\n<tr><td>past</td><td>たかかったです</td><td>しずかでした</td></tr>\n<tr><td>past negative</td><td>たかくなかったです</td><td>しずかじゃなかったです</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The most common wrong answer is しずかかった for the past, made by copying the い-adjective pattern. The second most common is たかいじゃないです, made by copying the な-adjective pattern. The two families never share a pattern.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Choose the right particle: (a) がっこう __ べんきょうします (b) がっこう __ いきます (c) パン __ たべます (d) ともだち __ いきます (e) でんしゃ __ いきます.", "a": "<p>Ask what job the noun is doing, then pick the label.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) がっこう で べんきょうします (gakkō de benkyō shimasu). Study is an activity that happens at a place, so で.</li>\n<li>(b) がっこう に いきます (gakkō ni ikimasu). Going is movement towards a destination, so に. へ also works here.</li>\n<li>(c) パン を たべます (pan o tabemasu). Bread is the direct object of eating, so を.</li>\n<li>(d) ともだち と いきます (tomodachi to ikimasu). A person you go with takes と.</li>\n<li>(e) でんしゃ で いきます (densha de ikimasu). The means of transport takes で.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(a) and (b) are the pair that catches everyone: same place, different particle, because one is an activity and the other is movement. (c) and (e) both use で but for different reasons, place of action and means.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Translate into polite Japanese: (a) there is a cat under the table (b) there is a book on the desk (c) is there a bank near here? (d) my brother is not a teacher.", "a": "<p>For (a) and (b) use the existence pattern: place に thing が あります or います. For (d) use the noun-sentence negative.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) テーブル の した に ねこ が います (tēburu no shita ni neko ga imasu). A cat is alive, so います.</li>\n<li>(b) つくえ の うえ に ほん が あります (tsukue no ue ni hon ga arimasu). A book is not alive, so あります.</li>\n<li>(c) この ちかく に ぎんこう が あります か (kono chikaku ni ginkō ga arimasu ka). Question marker か at the very end, after the verb.</li>\n<li>(d) あに は せんせい じゃありません (ani wa sensei ja arimasen). Talking about my own brother, so the humble あに, and じゃありません for the negative.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Notice that (a) and (b) put the thing with が, not は. The は version (ほん は つくえ の うえ に あります) is also correct, but it answers a different question, \"where is the book\", not \"what is on the desk\".</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Fix each sentence: (a) わたし は たべます ごはん を (b) これ ほん です (c) がくせい が あります (d) しずかかったです (e) たべます です.", "a": "<p>Each one breaks a rule from this module.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) The verb must be last: わたし は ごはん を たべます (watashi wa gohan o tabemasu).</li>\n<li>(b) これ already means \"this thing\", so it cannot take another noun. Either これは ほん です (kore wa hon desu) or この ほん です (kono hon desu).</li>\n<li>(c) がくせい (gakusei, student) is a person, so it needs います: がくせい が います (gakusei ga imasu).</li>\n<li>(d) しずか is a な-adjective, so the past is しずかでした (shizuka deshita). しずかかった copies the い-adjective pattern and is wrong.</li>\n<li>(e) A verb already carries politeness in ます, so です cannot follow it. Just たべます (tabemasu).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>All five are high-frequency errors in N5 writing tasks. Read your own sentence backwards from the verb and each of them becomes visible.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "Read and translate: わたし の ちち は ぎんこう の しゃいん です。まいにち でんしゃ で おおさか に いきます。わたし の いえ に ねこ が ふたり います。ちち は ねこ が すきです。Explain why います is used and why が appears twice.", "a": "<p>Translation: my father is a bank employee. Every day he goes to Osaka by train. There are two cats at my house. My father likes cats.</p>\n<p>Why います: the cats are living creatures, so existence uses います rather than あります. Note also ふたり (futari), the irregular counter for two people, which Japanese also uses for two animals here, a common and acceptable usage at N5 level.</p>\n<p>Why が appears twice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The first が marks ねこ as the thing that exists, in the pattern place に thing が います. The cats are the new information in that sentence, which is exactly what が signals.</li>\n<li>The second が is the fixed particle of the すき pattern. ねこ が すきです means \"cats are likeable to me\". The thing you like takes が, not を, and this is not negotiable.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two more details worth noticing: わたし の いえ uses の twice over, once for possession and once inside いえ に for location; and ちち (chichi) is the humble word for my father, correct when speaking about him to someone else.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": "Semester_2/Japanese-ForeignLanguageProficiency/JP-M03.md"}, {"n": 4, "title": "The て-form & Elementary Patterns", "tagline": "One verb form that unlocks requests, permission, prohibition, ongoing actions and sequencing.", "minutes": 17, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>The て-form (て形, te-kei) is the single most useful shape in Japanese. It is not a tense and it is not polite or plain, it is a <em>connector</em>. It means \"and then\", or \"doing\", and a handful of endings attached after it turn one verb into requests, permission, prohibition and ongoing action.</p>\n<p>Learn the て-form once and you get six patterns for free: てください (please do), てもいいです (you may), てはいけません (you must not), ています (is doing), てから (after doing), and plain chaining of actions in a sentence.</p>\n<p>Here is the honest part: forming the て-form is pure memorisation of sound rules, and there is no shortcut that beats saying them aloud. But the rules are few and completely regular, with exactly one exception you will meet every day.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>N5 tests the polite register throughout, so the patterns below are given in です and ます form: てください, てもいいです, てはいけません, ています. Plain forms like てくれ and ている appear inside quoted speech in reading passages, so recognise them, but write the polite ones.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>You cannot build a て-form without knowing the verb's group, so keep the group test from the previous module in your head: Group 1 ends in a plain u-sound, Group 2 ends in -eru or -iru, Group 3 is する and 来る.</p>\n<p>The て-form attaches to the <em>stem</em> for Group 2 verbs, but it changes the final syllable for Group 1 verbs. That is the whole difficulty. Group 1 has five sub-rules, based on the last syllable of the dictionary form, and each sub-rule maps to one of four shapes: って, んで, いて, いで, して.</p>\n<p>One habit makes this fast: memorise the pairs as sounds, not spellings. む and ぶ and ぬ go to んで. う and つ and る go to って. く goes to いて, ぐ goes to いで, す goes to して. Say のみます、のんで、のみます、のんで until it is one reflex.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why it is called the て-form</span><p>Because every one of these forms literally ends in て (or で when the sound voices). The ending て is the hinge, and whatever you bolt onto the hinge decides the meaning. Once you see it that way, てください is just \"て + please\".</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.1", "title": "Building the て-form", "html": "<p>Work from the dictionary form and the group, never from the ます form. The ます form hides the sound you need.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Final syllable</th><th>て-form ending</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>う, つ, る</td><td>って</td><td>買う (kau) → 買って (katte); 待つ (matsu) → 待って (matte); 帰る (kaeru) → 帰って (kaette)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>む, ぶ, ぬ</td><td>んで</td><td>飲む (nomu) → 飲んで (nonde); 遊ぶ (asobu) → 遊んで (asonde)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>く</td><td>いて</td><td>書く (kaku) → 書いて (kaite); 聞く (kiku) → 聞いて (kiite)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>ぐ</td><td>いで</td><td>泳ぐ (oyogu) → 泳いで (oyoide)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>す</td><td>して</td><td>話す (hanasu) → 話して (hanashite)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>-eru, -iru</td><td>drop る, add て</td><td>食べる (taberu) → 食べて (tabete); 見る (miru) → 見て (mite)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>する, 来る</td><td>して, きて</td><td>する (suru) → して (shite); 来る (kuru) → 来て (kite)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Now the one exception that matters. 行く (iku, go) ends in く, so the rule predicts 行いて. That is wrong. 行く takes って and becomes 行って (itte). Every other く verb is regular, so 書いて and 聞いて are correct. Learn 行って as a fixed sound, the way you learnt an irregular English past tense.</p>\n<p>Two small notes that save marks. When the ending voices to で, the word still counts as a て-form: 飲んで (nonde) is a て-form verb, not a separate category. And いい (ii, good) is an adjective, so it has no て-form of its own; its linking form is よくて (yokute), built from the stem よく.</p>\n<p>Test yourself with a chain: おきる (okiru) → 起きて, たべる → 食べて, いく → 行って, のむ → 飲んで, はなす → 話して, する → して. If all six come out without hesitation, you own this form.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.2", "title": "てください: polite requests", "html": "<p>Attach ください (kudasai) to a て-form and you get \"please do this\". It is a request, not an order, and it is safe in almost every situation a student will meet.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">ここ に なまえ を かいて ください。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> koko ni namae o kaite kudasai, \"please write your name here\". なまえ (namae) name, かく (kaku) write.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Asking someone to do something for you. Polite enough for a stranger, a teacher or a form at a counter.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> すみません、ちょっと まって ください (sumimasen, chotto matte kudasai), excuse me, please wait a moment.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>The structure never varies: て-form first, ください last. みて ください (mite kudasai) please look, きいて ください (kiite kudasai) please listen, ゆっくり はなして ください (yukkuri hanashite kudasai) please speak slowly.</p>\n<p>Word order inside the request follows normal rules, so the object keeps its particle. ドア を しめて ください (doa o shimete kudasai) is \"please close the door\", with を on the door. And the place of an action keeps で or に: ここ で まって ください (koko de matte kudasai), please wait here.</p>\n<p>To make a request negative, that is \"please do not\", the pattern changes to ないで ください (naide kudasai), built from the plain negative. しんぱい しないで ください (shinpai shinaide kudasai) is \"please do not worry\". Recognise it in reading; the て-form patterns are the ones you will produce most.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why ください is separate</span><p>ください comes from くださる (kudasaru), a humble verb meaning \"to give me\". So てください is literally \"please give me the doing of this\". Japanese requests are grammatically gifts, which is why the pattern feels polite even without すみません in front.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.3", "title": "てもいいです and てはいけません", "html": "<p>These two are a pair, and both hang off the て-form. One gives permission, the other removes it.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">ここ に すわって も いいですか。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> koko ni suwatte mo ii desu ka, \"may I sit here?\" すわる (suwaru) sit.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Asking for permission. As a statement, てもいいです means \"you may\".</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> ペン を つかって も いいですか (pen o tsukatte mo ii desu ka), may I use your pen?</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>Read the pieces: て-form plus も (even) plus いい (good) plus です. So \"even if you do this, it is good\", which is exactly how permission is phrased. To refuse politely, the usual answer is not a flat no but すみません、ちょっと… (sumimasen, chotto…), which is how real Japanese says \"sorry, that is a bit difficult\".</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">ここ で たばこ を すって は いけません。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> koko de tabako o sutte wa ikemasen, \"you must not smoke here\". たばこ (tabako) tobacco, すう (suu) smoke.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Rules, notices and prohibitions. It is the language of signs and school regulations.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> しゃしん を とって は いけません (shashin o totte wa ikemasen), you must not take photographs.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>いけません (ikemasen) literally means \"it will not go\", so てはいけません is \"doing this will not go\", that is, it is not allowed. Both patterns appear in the same shape: て-form, then the particle は, then the verdict.</p>\n<div class=\"grid2\"><div><h4>Permission</h4><p>て + も + いいです = you may.<br>て + も + いいですか = may I?<br>Answer yes: はい、いいです。Answer no politely: すみません、ちょっと…</p></div><div><h4>Prohibition</h4><p>て + は + いけません = you must not.<br>Stronger spoken form: だめです (dame desu), no good.<br>Signs often use it without a subject at all.</p></div></div>\n<p>Watch the も and は. Permission uses も, prohibition uses は. Swapping them produces nonsense, and it is the single most common error in this module.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.4", "title": "ています: ongoing actions and states", "html": "<p>て-form plus います describes something in progress, like English \"-ing\". It is also used for states that continue, which is where English speakers get confused.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">いま ごはん を たべて います。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> ima gohan o tabete imasu, \"I am eating a meal now\". いま (ima) now, ごはん (gohan) rice or meal.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> An action happening at this moment, or a situation that is currently true.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> あめ が ふって います (ame ga futte imasu), it is raining.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>The progressive reading is the easy half: よんで います (yonde imasu) is reading, はたらいて います (hataraite imasu) is working, べんきょうして います (benkyō shite imasu) is studying.</p>\n<p>The second reading is <em>state</em>, and it does not translate as \"-ing\". とうきょう に すんで います (Tōkyō ni sunde imasu) means \"I live in Tokyo\", not \"I am living in Tokyo this week\". けっこん して います (kekkon shite imasu) means \"I am married\", a state, not an event. しごと を して います (shigoto o shite imasu) means \"I work at a job\", and it is how you state your occupation.</p>\n<p>The rule of thumb: if the verb describes something that, once started, keeps being true, ています gives a state. If it describes a repeatable activity, ています gives \"currently doing\".</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Using ています for the simple present. \"I eat rice every day\" is まいにち ごはん を たべます, not たべて います. ています means it is happening now or is an ongoing state, never a habit.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "4.5", "title": "Chaining actions: て and てから", "html": "<p>Because the て-form is a connector, you can put several actions in one sentence without repeating the subject. The last verb carries the tense and the politeness; every earlier verb sits in て-form.</p>\n<pre class=\"ascii\">あさ: おきて -> かお を あらって -> ごはん を たべて -> いきます</pre>\n<p>In full: あさ おきて、かお を あらって、ごはん を たべて、がっこう に いきます (asa okite, kao o aratte, gohan o tabete, gakkō ni ikimasu). Morning: get up, wash my face, eat breakfast, go to school. Only いきます is in ます form, because it is the last one.</p>\n<p>This is a chain of events in the order they happen, and the tense is decided once, at the end. If the whole chain is in the past, the final verb becomes いきました and the て-forms stay exactly as they are: あさ おきて、ごはん を たべて、がっこう に いきました.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">ごはん を たべて から、べんきょうします。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> gohan o tabete kara, benkyō shimasu, \"after eating a meal, I study\".</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> When the order matters and you want to stress \"after doing X, then Y\".</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> しごと が おわって から、うち に かえります (shigoto ga owatte kara, uchi ni kaerimasu), after work finishes, I go home.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>The difference between plain chaining and てから is emphasis. たべて、べんきょうします lists two things. たべて から、べんきょうします insists that the study comes after the meal. When a sequence matters, てから makes it explicit.</p>\n<p>One more use of the て-form deserves a mention: linking adjectives. この へや は ひろくて あかるいです (kono heya wa hirokute akarui desu) is \"this room is spacious and bright\", with ひろい (hiroi, spacious) becoming ひろくて. And for な-adjectives the linker is で: しずかで きれいな まち (shizuka de kirei na machi), a quiet and beautiful town.</p>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "The て-form rules and pattern table", "html": "<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">う / つ / る → って &nbsp;·&nbsp; む / ぶ / ぬ → んで</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The two big Group 1 families. The consonant of the ending decides which of the two shapes you get.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Most Group 1 verbs you will ever meet fall into one of these two.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> 買って (katte) buy, 待って (matte) wait, 帰って (kaette) return, 飲んで (nonde) drink, 遊んで (asonde) play.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">く → いて &nbsp;·&nbsp; ぐ → いで &nbsp;·&nbsp; す → して</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The remaining Group 1 endings. Note the voicing in ぐ to いで.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> All く, ぐ and す verbs, except 行く which takes って.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> 書いて (kaite) write, 泳いで (oyoide) swim, 話して (hanashite) speak, 行って (itte) go.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">Group 2: drop る add て &nbsp;·&nbsp; Group 3: して, きて</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The easy families. Nothing changes except the final る.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Verbs ending -eru or -iru, plus する and 来る. Remember 帰る is Group 1, not Group 2.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> 食べて (tabete) eat, 見て (mite) see, して (shite) do, 来て (kite) come.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>てください</td><td>please do</td><td>まって ください (matte kudasai) please wait</td></tr>\n<tr><td>てもいいです</td><td>you may do</td><td>はいって も いいです (haitte mo ii desu) you may come in</td></tr>\n<tr><td>てもいいですか</td><td>may I do?</td><td>しゃしん を とって も いいですか (may I take a photo?)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>てはいけません</td><td>you must not do</td><td>はいって は いけません (you must not enter)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>ています</td><td>is doing / is in a state</td><td>すんで います (sunde imasu) lives</td></tr>\n<tr><td>てから</td><td>after doing</td><td>たべて から (tabete kara) after eating</td></tr>\n<tr><td>て、て、ます</td><td>and then, and then, does</td><td>おきて、たべて、いきます (get up, eat, go)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — from dictionary form to a request</h4>\n<p>まつ (matsu, wait) is Group 1 ending in つ, so it takes って: まって. Add ください.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">ちょっと まって ください (chotto matte kudasai) — please wait a moment.</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — asking permission in a shop</h4>\n<p>You want to try on a shirt. きる (kiru, wear) is Group 2, so て-form is きて. Add も いいですか.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">この シャツ を きて も いいですか (kono shatsu o kite mo ii desu ka) — may I try this shirt on?</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 3 — a prohibition on a sign</h4>\n<p>The verb はいる (hairu, enter) is Group 1 despite ending in -iru, so て-form is はいって. Prohibition uses は, not も.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">はいって は いけません (haitte wa ikemasen) — do not enter.</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 4 — describing what you do now</h4>\n<p>Asked いま なに を して います か (ima nani o shite imasu ka), what are you doing now, you can answer with the same pattern.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">にほんご を べんきょうして います (nihongo o benkyō shite imasu) — I am studying Japanese.</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 5 — a morning chain</h4>\n<p>Four actions, three て-forms, one final verb carrying the tense. Past tense means only the last verb changes.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">おきて、シャワー を あびて、ごはん を たべて、でかけました (okite, shawā o abite, gohan o tabete, dekakemashita)</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Making 行く regular. 行く ends in く but takes って: 行って (itte), not 行いて. It is the only exception in the く family, and it is the most used verb in the language.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Swapping も and は in the permission and prohibition pair. てもいいです gives permission, てはいけません forbids. てもいけません and てはいいです are not usable patterns.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Building the て-form from the ます form. のみます does not show you んで. Go back to the dictionary form のむ, then apply the rule. Building from ます produces invented shapes like のみて.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Putting the whole chain in past tense. In おきて、たべて、いきました only the last verb is past. おきました、たべました、いきました is three separate sentences, not one chained action.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Using ています for a habit. まいにち べんきょうして います can mean \"I am studying every day at the moment\", but a plain habit is まいにち べんきょうします.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>て-form is a connector, not a tense: \"and then\" or \"doing\".</li>\n<li>Group 1: う つ る to って, む ぶ ぬ to んで, く to いて, ぐ to いで, す to して.</li>\n<li>行く is the one irregular: 行って, never 行いて.</li>\n<li>Group 2 drops る and adds て; Group 3 is して and きて.</li>\n<li>てください please do; てもいいですか may I; てはいけません you must not.</li>\n<li>ています is happening now or an ongoing state such as すんで います.</li>\n<li>てから stresses after doing; plain chaining keeps tense only on the final verb.</li>\n<li>Adjective linking: い-adjective to くて, な-adjective to で.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Give the て-form of: 飲む (nomu), 書く (kaku), 話す (hanasu), 食べる (taberu), 行く (iku), する (suru).", "a": "<p>Apply the group rule to the dictionary form each time.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>Rule</th><th>て-form</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>飲む (nomu)</td><td>Group 1, む to んで</td><td>飲んで (nonde)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>書く (kaku)</td><td>Group 1, く to いて</td><td>書いて (kaite)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>話す (hanasu)</td><td>Group 1, す to して</td><td>話して (hanashite)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>食べる (taberu)</td><td>Group 2, drop る</td><td>食べて (tabete)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>行く (iku)</td><td>Group 1, irregular</td><td>行って (itte)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>する (suru)</td><td>Group 3</td><td>して (shite)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Only 行く breaks a rule. If you wrote 行いて, mark it now and say 行って ten times aloud, because this verb appears in almost every N5 dialogue.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Turn these into polite requests with てください: (a) まつ (matsu, wait) (b) ゆっくり はなす (hanasu, speak) (c) なまえ を かく (kaku, write) (d) ドア を しめる (shimeru, close).", "a": "<p>Build the て-form, then attach ください at the very end.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) まって ください (matte kudasai) — please wait.</li>\n<li>(b) ゆっくり はなして ください (yukkuri hanashite kudasai) — please speak slowly.</li>\n<li>(c) ここ に なまえ を かいて ください (koko ni namae o kaite kudasai) — please write your name here.</li>\n<li>(d) ドア を しめて ください (doa o shimete kudasai) — please close the door.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The object keeps を and the place keeps に or で. Only the final verb shape changes, so the request never disturbs the rest of the sentence.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Say in Japanese: (a) may I use this pen? (b) you must not take photographs here (c) please do not worry.", "a": "<p>Permission is てもいいですか, prohibition is てはいけません, and the negative request is ないで ください.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) この ペン を つかって も いいですか (kono pen o tsukatte mo ii desu ka). つかう (tsukau) is Group 1 ending in う, so つかって.</li>\n<li>(b) ここ で しゃしん を とって は いけません (koko de shashin o totte wa ikemasen). とる (toru) is Group 1 ending in る, so とって. Prohibition takes は.</li>\n<li>(c) しんぱい しないで ください (shinpai shinaide kudasai). The negative request uses the plain negative しない plus で ください, not a て-form.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Note that (b) uses で after ここ, because taking photographs is an activity at a place, not movement towards it.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Translate: (a) I live in Delhi (b) my elder sister is married (c) it is raining now (d) I am working at a bank. Then explain why each uses ています.", "a": "<p>All four use ています, but for two different reasons.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) デリー に すんで います (Derī ni sunde imasu). すむ (sumu) is Group 1 ending in む, so すんで. This is a state: living somewhere is true continuously.</li>\n<li>(b) あね は けっこん して います (ane wa kekkon shite imasu). Also a state. Being married is not an action happening right now, it is a condition that holds.</li>\n<li>(c) いま あめ が ふって います (ima ame ga futte imasu). Progressive. The rain is falling at this moment. ふる (furu) is Group 1 ending in る, so ふって.</li>\n<li>(d) ぎんこう で はたらいて います (ginkō de hataraite imasu). Progressive or habitual depending on context, and it is the standard way to state your job. はたらく (hataraku) is Group 1 ending in く, so はたらいて.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The distinction: state verbs (live, be married, know, work somewhere as an occupation) use ています for an ongoing condition. Activity verbs (eat, read, rain) use ています for \"right now\".</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Fix the errors: (a) がっこう に 行いて います (b) ここ に すわって は いいですか (c) おきました、たべて、いきました (d) のみます て ください.", "a": "<p>Each sentence breaks one て-form rule.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) 行く is irregular, so the て-form is 行って: がっこう に 行って います (gakkō ni itte imasu).</li>\n<li>(b) Permission uses も, not は: ここ に すわって も いいですか (koko ni suwatte mo ii desu ka). With は it would need いけません to mean prohibition.</li>\n<li>(c) Only the last verb carries the past tense. Correct: おきて、たべて、いきました (okite, tabete, ikimashita). The first two stay in て-form.</li>\n<li>(d) The て-form is built from the dictionary form のむ, not from のみます, and it attaches without a gap: のんで ください (nonde kudasai).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(b) and (c) are the two that cost the most marks, because both look almost right. Read the particle and the tense of every verb separately before submitting.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "A sign in a library reads: ここ で はなして は いけません。ほん を かりて も いいです。しずか に して ください。Explain each pattern, then say what a student may and may not do.", "a": "<p>Translation: you must not talk here. You may borrow books. Please be quiet.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Sentence</th><th>Pattern</th><th>Force</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>はなして は いけません</td><td>て-form plus は いけません</td><td>prohibition, talking is forbidden</td></tr>\n<tr><td>かりて も いいです</td><td>て-form plus も いいです</td><td>permission, borrowing is allowed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>しずか に して ください</td><td>て-form plus ください</td><td>request, please be quiet</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>So a student may borrow books and must not talk, and the third line is a polite request rather than a rule. Note that しずか に して is built from a な-adjective plus に する, meaning \"make it quiet\", which is a very common N5 phrase in classrooms.</p>\n<p>The exam angle: a reading question often puts a permission and a prohibition in the same short notice and asks which activity is allowed. Scan for ては いけません first, then ても いいです, then answer. The prohibition always wins if the two seem to overlap, because a specific ban overrides a general permission.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": "Semester_2/Japanese-ForeignLanguageProficiency/JP-M04.md"}, {"n": 5, "title": "Expressing Yourself — Likes, Abilities, Experience, Invitations, Opinions", "tagline": "Turn correct sentences into real conversation: what you like, what you can do, what you have done.", "minutes": 16, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Up to here you have been building correct sentences. This module is about having something to say. Six patterns cover almost all of it: what you do every day, what you like, what you can do, what you have done before, how to invite someone, and how to give a simple opinion.</p>\n<p>Two of these patterns share one small particle, が, and two of them share one small word, こと. Learn those two carriers and the six patterns stop being six things to memorise and become one idea with variations.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why these six</span><p>They are the six things N5 conversation actually needs. Almost every N5 dialogue is two people finding out what the other likes, can do, has done, and wants to do next. If you can handle those, you can hold a beginner conversation and you can answer most speaking prompts.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>Before these patterns make sense, hold three facts steady.</p>\n<p><strong>Liking and ability take が, not を.</strong> すき (suki, likeable), きらい (kirai, dislikeable), じょうず (jōzu, skilled), へた (heta, unskilled) and わかる (wakaru, understand) are all treated as descriptions of the thing, not actions on it. So ねこ が すきです (neko ga suki desu), cats are likeable to me.</p>\n<p><strong>こと turns a verb into a noun.</strong> こと (koto) means \"thing, matter\". Put it after a plain verb and the whole action becomes a noun you can talk about: よむ こと (yomu koto) is \"the act of reading\". This is how ability and experience work.</p>\n<p><strong>These patterns take plain forms, not ます.</strong> ことが できます and たことが あります both attach to the plain form: よむ こと, よんだ こと. Keep the politeness on です or ます at the end, not inside the pattern.</p>\n<p>One more: すき and じょうず behave like な-adjectives, so すきな ほん (suki na hon) is \"a book I like\" and じょうずな ひと (jōzu na hito) is \"a skilled person\".</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.1", "title": "Daily activities and the shape of your day", "html": "<p>Describing a routine is mostly time words plus ます forms, with て-form chains holding the actions together. The three time markers you need are まえ (mae, before), あと (ato, after) and それから (sorekara, after that).</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">まいにち しちじ に おきて、がっこう に いきます。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> mainichi shichiji ni okite, gakkō ni ikimasu, \"every day I get up at seven and go to school\".</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Any routine sentence. に marks the clock time, and the て-form chains the second action.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> ろくじ に おきて、ごはん を たべて、でんしゃ に のります (rokuji ni okite, gohan o tabete, densha ni norimasu).</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>The standard routine vocabulary is small: おきます (okimasu, get up), かお を あらいます (kao o araimasu, wash my face), シャワー を あびます (shawā o abimasu, take a shower), あさごはん を たべます (asagohan o tabemasu, eat breakfast), でかけます (dekakemasu, go out), はたらきます (hatarakimasu, work), うち に かえります (uchi ni kaerimasu, go home), ばんごはん を たべます (bangohan o tabemasu, eat dinner), ねます (nemasu, sleep).</p>\n<p>Time words slot in before the verb and need no particle: きょう (kyō, today), あした (ashita, tomorrow), きのう (kinō, yesterday), まいあさ (maiasa, every morning), まいばん (maiban, every night), らいしゅう (raishū, next week), せんしゅう (senshū, last week). Clock times and dates do take に: しちじ に (shichiji ni, at seven), げつようび に (getsuyōbi ni, on Monday).</p>\n<p>For \"before\" and \"after\" with a verb, use まえ に and てから. ねる まえ に ほん を よみます (neru mae ni hon o yomimasu) is \"I read before sleeping\", and ばんごはん を たべて から べんきょうします (bangohan o tabete kara benkyō shimasu) is \"I study after dinner\".</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.2", "title": "Likes and dislikes", "html": "<p>The core pattern is simple and takes が: the thing you like is marked with が, and すきです follows.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">わたし は ねこ が すきです。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> watashi wa neko ga suki desu, \"I like cats\". Literally \"as for me, cats are likeable\".</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Stating a preference about a noun. Swap すき for きらい to dislike.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> わたし は コーヒー が すきです (watashi wa kōhī ga suki desu), I like coffee.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>Because すき is an adjective-like word, not a verb, you can grade it and negate it with です.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Strength</th><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>love</td><td>だいすきです (daisuki desu)</td><td>えいが が だいすきです (I love films)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>like</td><td>すきです (suki desu)</td><td>おんがく が すきです (I like music)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>do not like much</td><td>あまり すきじゃないです (amari suki ja nai desu)</td><td>からい たべもの が あまり すきじゃないです</td></tr>\n<tr><td>dislike</td><td>きらいです (kirai desu)</td><td>さむい ひ が きらいです (I dislike cold days)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>asking</td><td>なに が すきですか (nani ga suki desu ka)</td><td>what do you like?</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two things to be careful with. First, きらい is a strong word, closer to \"cannot stand\" than \"not my favourite\", so in polite conversation the soft version あまり すきじゃないです is better. Second, the object takes が even though the English sentence looks like it wants an object. すし を すきです is wrong.</p>\n<p>For verbs, \"liking to do something\" uses こと: ほん を よむ こと が すきです (hon o yomu koto ga suki desu), I like reading books. Now こと carries the が, and the verb stays plain.</p>\n<p>There is a related pair for skill rather than taste: じょうず (jōzu, good at) and へた (heta, bad at), also taking が. かれ は うた が じょうずです (kare wa uta ga jōzu desu) is \"he is good at singing\". Use じょうず for other people, and for yourself use とくい (tokui, my strong point) or simply できます.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.3", "title": "Abilities: ことが できます", "html": "<p>To say \"I can do X\", take a plain verb, add こと, and finish with が できます.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">わたし は にほんご を はなす こと が できます。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> watashi wa nihongo o hanasu koto ga dekimasu, \"I can speak Japanese\".</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Stating an ability with any verb. Negative: できません.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> すし を つくる こと が できます (sushi o tsukuru koto ga dekimasu), I can make sushi.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>Note the two particles doing different jobs. を marks what is spoken, が marks the ability itself. にほんご が はなせます and にほんご を はなす こと が できます mean the same thing, and both are fine.</p>\n<p>できます also works directly with a noun, where it means \"is available\" or \"is possible\". ここ で カード が つかえます (koko de kādo ga tsukaemasu) is \"cards can be used here\", and クレジットカード は できますか (kurejitto kādo wa dekimasu ka) is \"do you take credit cards\".</p>\n<p>There is a shorter route too: the potential form of the verb itself. Group 1 verbs change the final syllable to the e-row plus る, so のむ becomes のめる and かく becomes かける. Group 2 verbs add られる, so たべる becomes たべられる. する becomes できる and 来る becomes こられる.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Dictionary</th><th>Potential</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>読む (yomu)</td><td>読める (yomeru)</td><td>can read</td></tr>\n<tr><td>書く (kaku)</td><td>書ける (kakeru)</td><td>can write</td></tr>\n<tr><td>話す (hanasu)</td><td>話せる (hanaseru)</td><td>can speak</td></tr>\n<tr><td>食べる (taberu)</td><td>食べられる (taberareru)</td><td>can eat</td></tr>\n<tr><td>する (suru)</td><td>できる (dekiru)</td><td>can do</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>For N5, ことが できます is the safest form to write, because it never depends on knowing the verb group. Use the potential form to recognise, and use ことが できます to produce.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.4", "title": "Experience: たことが あります", "html": "<p>To say \"I have done this before\", take the plain <em>past</em> form of the verb, add こと, and finish with が あります.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">にほん に いった こと が あります。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> nihon ni itta koto ga arimasu, \"I have been to Japan\". いった is the plain past of 行く.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Any past experience, with no time stated. If you say when, use the ordinary past instead.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> すし を たべた こと が あります (sushi o tabeta koto ga arimasu), I have eaten sushi.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>The plain past forms you need: いった (itta, went), たべた (tabeta, ate), みた (mita, saw), よんだ (yonda, read), した (shita, did), きた (kita, came). Notice these are the same て-form sounds with た or だ instead of て or で: 行って becomes 行った, 飲んで becomes 飲んだ. If you know the て-form, you already know this.</p>\n<p>Negative experience is ありません: にほん に いった こと が ありません (nihon ni itta koto ga arimasen), I have never been to Japan. To ask, use ありますか: うま に のった こと が ありますか (uma ni notta koto ga arimasu ka), have you ever ridden a horse?</p>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Using たことが あります with a specific time. きのう すし を たべた こと が あります is wrong, because \"yesterday\" is a specific occasion, not an experience in general. Say きのう すし を たべました. The experience pattern is only for \"at some point in my life\".</p></div>\n<p>Also keep the two ことが patterns apart. よむ こと が できます is ability, with a plain non-past verb. よんだ こと が あります is experience, with a plain past verb. Same こと, different tense, completely different meaning.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.5", "title": "Invitations and suggestions", "html": "<p>Three endings cover inviting, proposing and offering. All three are polite and all three are safe with acquaintances.</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">いっしょ に えいが を みませんか。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> issho ni eiga o mimasen ka, \"won't you watch a film together?\"</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Inviting someone. The negative question is softer than a direct invitation, because it leaves room to refuse.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> あした コーヒー を のみませんか (ashita kōhī o nomimasen ka), shall we have coffee tomorrow?</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">いっしょ に いきましょう。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> issho ni ikimashō, \"let's go together\".</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Suggesting a joint action, especially when you already expect agreement.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> そろそろ かえりましょう (sorosoro kaerimashō), let's head home soon.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">てつだいましょうか。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> tetsudaimashō ka, \"shall I help?\" てつだう (tetsudau) is to help.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Offering to do something for the other person. The か makes it a question, so it is an offer, not a decision.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> にもつ を もちましょうか (nimotsu o mochimashō ka), shall I carry your luggage?</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>The difference is direction. ませんか invites the other person to join you. ましょう proposes that we both do it. ましょうか offers to do it for them. ましょう alone can also be your own decision announced aloud: わたし が いきましょう (watashi ga ikimashō), I will go.</p>\n<p>Answers matter as much as invitations. Accept with ええ、いいですね (ē, ii desu ne), that sounds good, or ぜひ (zehi), by all means. Decline politely with すみません、ちょっと… (sumimasen, chotto…), which is the standard soft no, or ざんねんですが (zannen desu ga), I am sorry but. A flat いやです is rude in this context.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "5.6", "title": "Simple opinions", "html": "<p>To give an opinion, quote your own thought with と おもいます (to omoimasu, I think).</p>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">にほんご は おもしろい と おもいます。</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> nihongo wa omoshiroi to omoimasu, \"I think Japanese is interesting\". おもしろい (omoshiroi) is interesting.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Any opinion. It softens a claim, which is why it is used constantly in polite conversation.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> この みせ は たかい と おもいます (kono mise wa takai to omoimasu), I think this shop is expensive.</div>\n</div></div>\n<p>The particle と here marks the content of your thought, and what comes before it must be a <em>plain</em> form. That gives three cases.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Before と おもいます</th><th>Form</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>い-adjective</td><td>plain, unchanged</td><td>たかい と おもいます (I think it is expensive)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>な-adjective or noun</td><td>add だ (da)</td><td>しずか だ と おもいます (I think it is quiet); がくせい だ と おもいます (I think he is a student)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>verb</td><td>plain form, not ます</td><td>あした あめ が ふる と おもいます (I think it will rain tomorrow)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>To ask for an opinion, say どう おもいますか (dō omoimasu ka), what do you think? To agree, use わたし も そう おもいます (watashi mo sō omoimasu), I think so too. To disagree gently: そう おもいません (sō omoimasen), I do not think so, which is far more polite than a blunt no.</p>\n<p>Reasons attach with から (kara, because), which comes after the reason clause: にほんご は おもしろい です から、まいにち べんきょうします (nihongo wa omoshiroi desu kara, mainichi benkyō shimasu), because Japanese is interesting, I study every day. から can follow a polite form, so it is one of the few connectors where です から is completely normal.</p>\n<p>For a stronger opinion, と おもいます can be replaced with と かんがえます (to kangaemasu, I consider), but for N5 stick with おもいます. It is the safest and the most common.</p>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "The six patterns in one place", "html": "<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">N が すきです / きらいです / じょうずです</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The thing liked, disliked or skilled at takes が, because these words describe the thing rather than act on it.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Preferences and skill, with a noun. Grade with だいすき or soften with あまり.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> おんがく が すきです (ongaku ga suki desu) I like music; うた が じょうずです (uta ga jōzu desu) good at singing.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">plain verb + こと が できます</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Ability. こと turns the action into a noun, and できます says it is possible.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Saying what you can do, with any verb, without needing its group.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> かんじ を よむ こと が できます (kanji o yomu koto ga dekimasu) I can read kanji.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">plain past verb + こと が あります</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Experience. \"There exists the fact that I did this.\"</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Past experience with no specific time mentioned. Negative is ありません.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> ふじさん に のぼった こと が あります (Fujisan ni nobotta koto ga arimasu) I have climbed Mount Fuji.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">ませんか / ましょう / ましょうか</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Invitation, joint proposal, and offer to do something for someone.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> ませんか to invite, ましょう to propose we both do it, ましょうか to offer help.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> いっしょ に いきませんか (issho ni ikimasen ka) won't you come with me?</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">plain clause + と おもいます</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> I think that ... The と quotes the thought, and the clause before it is plain.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Opinions. Add だ after a noun or な-adjective, and drop ます inside the quote.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> いい と おもいます (ii to omoimasu) I think it is good.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>I want to say</th><th>Pattern</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>I like X</td><td>X が すきです</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I can do X</td><td>X する こと が できます</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I have done X</td><td>X した こと が あります</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shall we do X?</td><td>X ませんか / X ましょう</td></tr>\n<tr><td>I think X</td><td>X と おもいます</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — a self-introduction that uses four patterns</h4>\n<p>わたし は ラーメン が すきです。すし を つくる こと が できます。インド の カレー も たべた こと が あります。にほん の たべもの は おいしい と おもいます。</p>\n<div class=\"res\">I like ramen. I can make sushi. I have also eaten Indian curry. I think Japanese food is delicious.</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — declining an invitation politely</h4>\n<p>Invitation: あした えいが を みませんか (ashita eiga o mimasen ka). Refusal: すみません、あした は しごと が あります。ざんねんですが、また こんど (sumimasen, ashita wa shigoto ga arimasu. zannen desu ga, mata kondo).</p>\n<div class=\"res\">Sorry, I have work tomorrow. What a pity, next time.</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 3 — experience versus a specific past</h4>\n<p>\"I have been to Kyoto\" is きょうと に いった こと が あります, an experience. \"I went to Kyoto last year\" is きょねん きょうと に いきました, a specific occasion. Both are past, only one is an experience.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">きょうと に いった こと が あります (experience) · きょねん きょうと に いきました (specific)</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 4 — offering help</h4>\n<p>You see a colleague carrying boxes. にもつ を もちましょうか (nimotsu o mochimashō ka), shall I carry your luggage? The answer おねがいします (onegaishimasu) means please do.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">にもつ を もちましょうか — shall I carry your luggage?</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Using を with すき, きらい, じょうず, へた or わかる. All five take が: ねこ が すきです, にほんご が わかります. ねこ を すきです is the classic beginner error.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Putting ます inside ことが できます or たことが あります. Correct: よむ こと が できます, よんだ こと が あります. Wrong: よみます こと が できます.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Confusing よむ こと が できます (can read) with よんだ こと が あります (have read). Same こと, but ability uses the plain non-past and experience uses the plain past.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Using the experience pattern with a stated time. きのう いった こと が あります is wrong because yesterday is a specific occasion. Use きのう いきました.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Saying あなた は じょうずです to someone's face about yourself. じょうず is for other people; describing your own skill with じょうず sounds boastful. Use できます or とくいです for yourself.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>すき, きらい, じょうず, へた, わかる all take が, never を.</li>\n<li>すき behaves like a な-adjective: すきな ほん, あまり すきじゃないです.</li>\n<li>Ability: plain verb + こと が できます. Negative できません.</li>\n<li>Experience: plain past verb + こと が あります. Never with a stated time.</li>\n<li>ませんか invites, ましょう proposes, ましょうか offers to help.</li>\n<li>Opinion: plain clause + と おもいます, with だ after nouns and な-adjectives.</li>\n<li>Reasons use から, and です から is perfectly normal.</li>\n<li>Soft no: すみません、ちょっと… ; soft agreement: いいですね.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Say in Japanese: (a) I like music (b) I dislike cold days (c) I love films (d) I do not like spicy food much.", "a": "<p>All four use が before the preference word, and grade it with a prefix or an adverb.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) わたし は おんがく が すきです (watashi wa ongaku ga suki desu).</li>\n<li>(b) わたし は さむい ひ が きらいです (watashi wa samui hi ga kirai desu). さむい (samui) is a い-adjective, so it sits bare before ひ (hi, day).</li>\n<li>(c) わたし は えいが が だいすきです (watashi wa eiga ga daisuki desu). だい (dai) is the intensifier prefix.</li>\n<li>(d) わたし は からい たべもの が あまり すきじゃないです (watashi wa karai tabemono ga amari suki ja nai desu).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Every one of these would be wrong with を. The が is not optional, because すき and きらい describe the thing rather than act on it.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Give the plain past form and then make an experience sentence for: 行く (iku), 食べる (taberu), 見る (miru), する (suru).", "a": "<p>The plain past follows the same sound rules as the て-form, with た or だ instead of て or で.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Verb</th><th>Plain past</th><th>Experience sentence</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>行く (iku)</td><td>行った (itta)</td><td>にほん に 行った こと が あります</td></tr>\n<tr><td>食べる (taberu)</td><td>食べた (tabeta)</td><td>すし を 食べた こと が あります</td></tr>\n<tr><td>見る (miru)</td><td>見た (mita)</td><td>ふじさん を 見た こと が あります</td></tr>\n<tr><td>する (suru)</td><td>した (shita)</td><td>にほんご で スピーチ を した こと が あります</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>行った is the irregular one, following 行って. Note that the こと stays in kana here; there is no need to write 事 at N5 unless you are confident of it.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Invite a friend to lunch tomorrow, then decline a different invitation politely. Write both dialogues in Japanese.", "a": "<p>Invitation uses ませんか, and a polite decline uses すみません、ちょっと… plus a reason.</p>\n<p>Dialogue 1, inviting:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A: あした いっしょ に ひるごはん を たべませんか (ashita issho ni hirugohan o tabemasen ka) — shall we have lunch together tomorrow?</li>\n<li>B: ええ、いいですね。なんじ が いいですか (ē, ii desu ne. nanji ga ii desu ka) — yes, sounds good. What time is good?</li>\n<li>A: じゅうにじ は どうですか (jūniji wa dō desu ka) — how about twelve?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Dialogue 2, declining:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A: どようび に えいが を みませんか (doyōbi ni eiga o mimasen ka) — shall we see a film on Saturday?</li>\n<li>B: すみません、どようび は アルバイト が あります。ざんねんですが、また こんど (sumimasen, doyōbi wa arubaito ga arimasu. zannen desu ga, mata kondo) — sorry, I have a part-time job on Saturday. What a pity, next time.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice that the refusal gives a reason and offers a future opening. A bare いやです would be socially wrong even though it is grammatically fine.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Translate: (a) I can read kanji (b) I cannot speak Chinese (c) I have never ridden a horse (d) have you ever eaten sushi?", "a": "<p>Ability uses plain verb plus こと が できます, experience uses plain past plus こと が あります.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>(a) かんじ を よむ こと が できます (kanji o yomu koto ga dekimasu).</li>\n<li>(b) ちゅうごくご を はなす こと が できません (chūgokugo o hanasu koto ga dekimasen). Negative goes on できます, not on the verb.</li>\n<li>(c) うま に のった こと が ありません (uma ni notta koto ga arimasen). のる (noru) is Group 1, so plain past is のった.</li>\n<li>(d) すし を たべた こと が ありますか (sushi o tabeta koto ga arimasu ka). Question marker at the very end.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The mistake to avoid in (b) is はなさない こと が できます, which says \"I can not-speak Chinese\". The negation belongs on できません.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Give your opinion in Japanese about learning Japanese, with one reason. Then say what you think about the statement にほんご は むずかしいです, using the correct form before と おもいます.", "a": "<p>Opinion uses a plain clause before と おもいます, and a reason attaches with から.</p>\n<p>Your opinion, for example: にほんご の べんきょう は おもしろい と おもいます。かんじ は むずかしい です から、まいにち れんしゅうします (nihongo no benkyō wa omoshiroi to omoimasu. kanji wa muzukashii desu kara, mainichi renshū shimasu). I think studying Japanese is interesting. Because kanji is difficult, I practise every day.</p>\n<p>Now the second task, which is the one being tested. The sentence given is にほんご は むずかしいです. Before と おもいます the です must disappear, because the quote is a plain clause. むずかしい is an い-adjective, so it stays unchanged.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">にほんご は むずかしい と おもいます (nihongo wa muzukashii to omoimasu) — I think Japanese is difficult.</div>\n<p>If the clause had been a noun or a な-adjective, you would need だ: にほんご は たいへん だ と おもいます (nihongo wa taihen da to omoimasu), I think Japanese is tough. Dropping that だ, or keeping です, are the two errors examiners look for.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "A short passage: わたし は インド の がくせい です。にほん の アニメ が だいすきです から、にほんご を べんきょうして います。ひらがな と カタカナ を よむ こと が できます。でも、かんじ は まだ あまり わかりません。にほん に いった こと が ありません。いつか いきたい と おもいます。Answer in English: what can this student do, what can they not do, and what do they want?", "a": "<p>Translation first: I am a student from India. Because I love Japanese anime, I am studying Japanese. I can read hiragana and katakana. But I still do not understand kanji much. I have never been to Japan. I think I want to go someday.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer from the passage</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>What can they do?</td><td>Read hiragana and katakana — よむ こと が できます</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What can they not do?</td><td>Understand kanji well — かんじ は まだ あまり わかりません</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What have they not done?</td><td>Never been to Japan — いった こと が ありません</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What do they want?</td><td>To go to Japan someday — いつか いきたい と おもいます</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The grammar worth noticing: だいすきです から gives the reason before the result, でも (demo, but) opens the contrast, まだ (mada, still) with a negative means \"not yet\", and いきたい is the desire form of いきます used inside と おもいます. Reading questions at N5 almost always ask exactly these four things, so train yourself to scan for できます, ません, ことが あります and と おもいます.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": null}, {"n": 6, "title": "The Four Skills & JLPT N5 Strategy", "tagline": "How the four skills are tested, what each one rewards, and how to practise them without wasting time.", "minutes": 14, "sections": [{"kind": "big", "num": "", "title": "Big picture, 30 seconds", "html": "<p>Everything in modules 1 to 5 exists to serve four skills: reading, listening, speaking and writing. N5 tests all of them, but it tests them through a narrow set of everyday situations, so the preparation is much more targeted than it looks.</p>\n<p>The scope is deliberately small: familiar topics, practical conversations, short passages, notices, and simple writing tasks. Nothing abstract, nothing literary, no long argument. If a passage is about a train timetable, a shop notice or someone's weekend, you are inside the syllabus. If it is about politics or philosophy, you are not.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--exam\"><span class=\"tag\">How it is asked</span><p>The test is organised as a written paper covering vocabulary, grammar and reading, plus a separate listening paper. The exact timings, the number of questions and the pass marks are set by the exam body and are not fixed by your syllabus, so check the official JLPT information for your attempt date rather than trusting a number you heard from a senior.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "basics", "num": "", "title": "Basics you must know first", "html": "<p>Four honest observations before you plan your study.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading is the bottleneck, not grammar.</strong> Most students know enough grammar by the end and still lose marks because they read kana too slowly. If you sound out がっこう one mora at a time, you will run out of time on the reading paper no matter how good your grammar is.</p>\n<p><strong>Listening rewards prediction, not translation.</strong> You do not have time to translate each sentence into English. You need to know what information is being asked for, then listen for that one piece: a time, a price, a place, a yes or no.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking is not on the N5 paper.</strong> The JLPT does not test speaking. Your syllabus still lists it, and it is worth practising because speaking is what makes the other three skills stick. Treat it as a study method, not an exam section.</p>\n<p><strong>Writing at N5 means short and correct.</strong> A few sentences on a familiar topic, with consistent です and ます and correct particles, beats a long ambitious paragraph full of errors.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.1", "title": "Reading: notices, short passages, kana to kanji", "html": "<p>N5 reading has a small number of recurring formats, and each one rewards a different habit.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>What it looks like</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Notice or sign</td><td>a short instruction, often with てください or てはいけません</td><td>find the verb and the permission or ban word first</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Short passage</td><td>three to six sentences about someone's day, family or plans</td><td>find who, when, where, what</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Kana to kanji</td><td>a word in kana, choose the kanji, or the reverse</td><td>read the whole word, not the single character</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Underlined reference</td><td>これ or それ, and what it points to</td><td>look at the sentence immediately before</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>The single most useful reading habit is to <em>find the verb first</em>. Because Japanese puts the verb last, the last word of a clause tells you whether something is a request, a prohibition, a past event or a plan. A notice that ends in てください is asking; one that ends in てはいけません is forbidding. Same place, opposite answer.</p>\n<p>The second habit is to mark time and place words. あした (ashita, tomorrow), きのう (kinō, yesterday), まいにち (mainichi, every day), に (ni, at), で (de, in) are the words the questions turn on. A typical item asks \"when will the person go\" and the passage says あした は しごと が あります から、あさって いきます (ashita wa shigoto ga arimasu kara, asatte ikimasu), because I have work tomorrow, I will go the day after. Miss あさって (asatte, the day after tomorrow) and you pick the wrong option.</p>\n<p>Speed comes from volume, not from technique. Read fifty short N5-level passages and your eye starts taking かな in chunks instead of one beat at a time. That is the whole secret.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.2", "title": "Listening: what the paper rewards", "html": "<p>Listening is where preparation pays off most, because the question types repeat and the traps are predictable.</p>\n<p>The situations are everyday: asking directions, buying something, arranging to meet, describing a routine, asking permission. The audio is played at natural speed but with clear pronunciation, and the speakers are usually two people, one of whom corrects the other.</p>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">1</span><div><p>Read the options before the audio starts, if the paper allows it. You are looking for the category of answer: a time, a place, a price, a number, a yes or no.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">2</span><div><p>Listen for the correction, not the first number. A very common pattern is さんじ ですか… いえ、よじ に しましょう (sanji desu ka… ie, yoji ni shimashō), three o'clock… no, let us make it four. The answer is four.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">3</span><div><p>Write the number as digits immediately, in kana or numerals, next to the option you think it belongs to. Holding numbers in your head while the next sentence plays is how people lose marks.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">4</span><div><p>Watch the negative at the end. いきません, できません and あまり すきじゃないです flip the meaning of everything before them.</p></div></div></div>\n<p>One practical warning: the distractors are near-misses, not random. しちじ against いちじ, よっか against ようか, さんびゃく against さんひゃく. That is why module 2 spent time on the irregular readings. They are exactly what the listening paper tests.</p>\n<div class=\"note note--why\"><span class=\"tag\">Why prediction beats translation</span><p>Translation is too slow for speech. If you know the question asks for a time, you can ignore most of the sentence and grab the one number. Prediction turns a translation task into a search task, and search is fast.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.3", "title": "Speaking: the cheapest way to fix everything else", "html": "<p>The JLPT does not test speaking, but speaking is the fastest way to improve reading and listening, because it forces you to process sound instead of symbols.</p>\n<p>Start with one fixed self-introduction you can say without thinking. It should cover name, where you are from, what you study, what you like, and one thing you can do. Something like: はじめまして。わたし は ラヴィ です。インド から きました。B.Tech の がくせい です。おんがく が すきです。にほんご を すこし はなす こと が できます。よろしく おねがいします (hajimemashite. watashi wa Ravi desu. Indo kara kimashita. B.Tech no gakusei desu. ongaku ga suki desu. nihongo o sukoshi hanasu koto ga dekimasu. yoroshiku onegaishimasu).</p>\n<p>Then use three drills, twenty minutes a day.</p>\n<div class=\"grid2\"><div><h4>Shadowing</h4><p>Play a short audio and repeat it a beat behind, copying the rhythm. Do not pause between sentences. This trains the mora timing that makes you understandable, and it trains your ear at the same time.</p></div><div><h4>Talking to yourself</h4><p>Narrate your day in ます form out loud: いま ごはん を たべて います。あした がっこう に いきます。It feels silly and it works, because you are producing the patterns instead of recognising them.</p></div></div>\n<p>Pronunciation points that matter most at this level: keep the five vowels flat and short, give every mora equal length, and do not stress syllables the way English does. Japanese rhythm is even, like a metronome, not bouncy. A long vowel really is two beats, so おばあさん takes one beat longer than おばさん and you must hear the difference in yourself.</p>\n<p>Record yourself once a week reading a short passage aloud. Comparing week one with week four is more motivating than any score, and it exposes the kana you are still slow on.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.4", "title": "Writing: short tasks done cleanly", "html": "<p>At N5, writing means short, correct, polite text: a self-introduction, a short message, a description of your day, a postcard-style note. The marks come from accuracy and consistency, not from length or cleverness.</p>\n<p>Run this checklist on every sentence you write, in this order.</p>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">1</span><div><p>Is the verb last? If anything follows the verb except か, move it.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">2</span><div><p>Does every noun have the right particle? は for topic, が for subject and for すき, を for object, に for destination and clock time, で for place of action and means.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">3</span><div><p>Is the register consistent? Pick です and ます and stay there. Mixing だ with です in the same paragraph is the most common N5 writing error.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">4</span><div><p>Are the tenses right? Remember that the present form covers the future, so あした いきます is correct and needs no future tense.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">5</span><div><p>Are your kana lengths right? Small っ, long vowels and ん are all full beats, and each one is a mark waiting to be lost.</p></div></div></div>\n<p>A safe model answer for \"write about your day\" runs five sentences: わたし は まいにち しちじ に おきます。あさごはん を たべて から、がっこう に いきます。ごご は としょかん で べんきょうします。よる は あまり テレビ を みません。ねる まえ に にほんご を べんきょうします。Every sentence is plain grammar you already own, and nothing in it can go badly wrong.</p>"}, {"kind": "concept", "num": "6.5", "title": "A plan that actually fits a semester", "html": "<p>Six weeks of steady work beats three months of good intentions. Here is a split that follows the modules you have just read.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Focus</th><th>Daily 45 minutes</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>kana to automatic</td><td>15 min kana reading aloud, 15 min writing kana, 15 min vocabulary</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>kanji and numbers</td><td>10 new kanji a day with one word each, counters and time drills</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>noun sentences and verbs</td><td>conjugate 10 verbs a day, write 5 sentences with them</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>adjectives and particles</td><td>particle fill-in exercises, describe three things around you</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>て-form and patterns</td><td>build 20 て-forms daily, then use each pattern twice</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>the six expression patterns</td><td>one short reading passage and one listening clip a day, plus a written paragraph</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Keep an error log. One page, two columns: what you got wrong, and the rule that would have prevented it. Review the log every Sunday. Most students make the same eight or ten mistakes for months; a log converts them into a checklist and the checklist stops them.</p>\n<p>Two study habits worth naming because they are counter-intuitive. First, review beats new material: spend a third of every session on things you already met, because forgetting is faster than learning. Second, always study in context, in whole sentences. すき is not a word you can use until you have said ねこ が すきです out loud twenty times.</p>\n<p>Finally, take one full mock paper under timed conditions before the exam, even if you score badly. The point is not the score, it is discovering that you run out of time on reading, which is a fixable problem you want to find early rather than on the day.</p>"}, {"kind": "formula", "num": "", "title": "Rules of thumb for each skill", "html": "<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">reading: find the last verb of each clause first</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The verb carries tense, politeness and the sentence type, so it decides what the clause is doing.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Every passage and notice, before you try to translate anything.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> ここ で たばこ を すって は いけません ends in a prohibition, so the answer to \"can I smoke here\" is no.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">listening: expect a correction after the first answer</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> The first number or plan mentioned is often replaced a sentence later.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Any dialogue with a time, price, place or quantity.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> にじ は どうですか… すみません、さんじ に してください. The answer is three, not two.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">writing: one register per paragraph, です and ます</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Consistent politeness reads as correct even when the grammar is simple.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Every written answer, message and self-introduction.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> がくせい です。にほんご を べんきょうします。おんがく が すきです。All three match.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"fml\"><div class=\"fml-eq\">speaking: every mora gets one equal beat</div>\n<div class=\"fml-meta\">\n<div><span class=\"k\">Meaning</span> Japanese rhythm is even. Length, not stress, carries meaning.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">When to use</span> Reading aloud, shadowing and any spoken answer.</div>\n<div><span class=\"k\">Tiny example</span> おばさん (obasan) aunt, four beats; おばあさん (obaasan) grandmother, five beats.</div>\n</div></div>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>Best single drill</th><th>Time per day</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Reading</td><td>one short passage, verb first, then translate</td><td>15 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Listening</td><td>one clip, note only the asked-for information</td><td>15 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speaking</td><td>shadowing, then narrate your day aloud</td><td>10 min</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Writing</td><td>five sentences with one grammar point</td><td>10 min</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>"}, {"kind": "example", "num": "", "title": "Solved examples", "html": "<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 1 — a notice, read the right way</h4>\n<p>としょかん で は たべもの を たべて は いけません。ほん は さんさつ まで かりる こと が できます。</p>\n<p>Find the verbs: たべて は いけません is a prohibition, かりる こと が できます is a permission. So eating is banned, borrowing up to three books is allowed.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">No eating in the library; you may borrow up to three books.</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 2 — a listening item with a correction</h4>\n<p>Audio roughly: あした の パーティー は しちじ から です。あ、すみません、はちじ から に なりました。Question: what time does the party start?</p>\n<p>The first time is しちじ (seven), then it is corrected to はちじ (eight). The answer is eight o'clock, and the trap is the first number.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">はちじ (hachiji) — eight o'clock</div></div>\n<div class=\"card\"><h4>Example 3 — a five-sentence writing answer</h4>\n<p>Task: write about your family. わたし の かぞく は よにん です。ちち と はは と いもうと が います。ちち は ぎんこういん です。はは は せんせい です。いもうと は がくせい です。よにん is four people, and every sentence ends in です.</p>\n<div class=\"res\">My family has four people. There are my father, my mother and my younger sister. My father is a bank employee, my mother is a teacher, my sister is a student.</div></div>"}, {"kind": "trap", "num": "", "title": "Common traps and mistakes", "html": "<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Believing a pass mark or question count you heard from a senior. Those numbers are set by the exam body, they can change, and your syllabus does not state them. Check the official JLPT information for your attempt.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Translating the listening audio word by word. You will fall behind by the second sentence. Listen for the category of answer instead: time, place, price, yes or no.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Answering the first number you hear. N5 dialogues very often correct themselves a sentence later, so hold your answer until the dialogue ends.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Mixing plain and polite forms in one written answer. Pick です and ます and keep them. A simple paragraph in one register scores better than a clever one that switches halfway.</p></div>\n<div class=\"note note--trap\"><span class=\"tag\">Common trap</span><p>Practising only recognition. Reading and listening practice alone will not fix your production. Say and write the patterns too, or the same errors survive every mock test.</p></div>"}, {"kind": "revision", "num": "", "title": "Quick revision box", "html": "<ul>\n<li>Four skills, one narrow scope: familiar topics, short passages, notices, simple writing.</li>\n<li>Timings, question counts and pass marks are set by the exam body, not by your syllabus.</li>\n<li>Reading: find the last verb of the clause, then the time and place words.</li>\n<li>Listening: predict the category of answer, expect a correction, write numbers down at once.</li>\n<li>Speaking is not on the N5 paper but it is the fastest way to improve the other three.</li>\n<li>Writing: short, polite, one register, correct particles, verb last.</li>\n<li>Keep an error log and review it weekly; review beats new material.</li>\n<li>Do one timed mock paper early so timing problems surface while they are fixable.</li>\n</ul>"}], "practice": [{"q": "Name the four skills your syllabus lists and say which ones the JLPT N5 paper actually tests.", "a": "<p>Your syllabus lists reading, listening, speaking and writing.</p>\n<p>The N5 paper itself has a written section covering vocabulary, grammar and reading, and a separate listening section. Speaking is not tested, and extended writing is not tested as a free composition either, though short written accuracy shows up in the grammar and reading questions.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Skill</th><th>On the N5 paper?</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Reading</td><td>yes, in the written paper</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Listening</td><td>yes, as a separate paper</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speaking</td><td>no, but it accelerates the others</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Writing</td><td>only indirectly, through accuracy in the written paper</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>Note the honest limit here: the number of questions, the timings and the pass marks are decided by the exam body, and your syllabus does not state them. Do not quote a figure you have not verified on the official source.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "Read this notice and answer: what may you do and what may you not do? えき の なか で しゃしん を とって は いけません。でんしゃ の なか で たべる こと が できます。", "a": "<p>Find the two verbs first, because they decide everything.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>とって は いけません is て-form plus prohibition, so taking photographs inside the station is forbidden.</li>\n<li>たべる こと が できます is ability or permission with こと, so eating inside the train is allowed.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"res\">You may not take photographs in the station; you may eat inside the train.</div>\n<p>The vocabulary worth confirming: えき (eki) station, なか (naka) inside, しゃしん (shashin) photograph, でんしゃ (densha) train, たべる (taberu) eat. Note that の なか で uses で, because it is the place where an action happens, not a destination.</p>", "tag": "easy"}, {"q": "A listening dialogue says: あした の ミーティング は くじ から です。あ、すみません、じゅうじ に なりました。Where should you look for the trap, and what is the answer?", "a": "<p>The dialogue states a time, then corrects it. That self-correction is the standard N5 listening trap.</p>\n<p>First time heard: くじ から です (kuji kara desu), from nine. Then じゅうじ に なりました (jūji ni narimashita), it has become ten. に なりました is the giveaway phrase, it means \"has changed to\".</p>\n<div class=\"res\">じゅうじ (jūji) — ten o'clock</div>\n<p>The habit that saves you: do not commit to an answer the moment you hear a number. Write both down with a mark, keep listening to the end, and let the last stated time win. The distractor is always the first number, and it is always said clearly, which is why people fall for it.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Write a five-sentence paragraph about your daily routine in polite Japanese, then list the particles you used and why.", "a": "<p>A model answer, five sentences, one register throughout:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>わたし は まいにち しちじ に おきます (watashi wa mainichi shichiji ni okimasu). I get up at seven every day.</li>\n<li>あさごはん を たべて から、がっこう に いきます (asagohan o tabete kara, gakkō ni ikimasu). After breakfast, I go to school.</li>\n<li>ごご は としょかん で べんきょうします (gogo wa toshokan de benkyō shimasu). In the afternoon I study at the library.</li>\n<li>よる は あまり テレビ を みません (yoru wa amari terebi o mimasen). At night I do not watch much television.</li>\n<li>ねる まえ に にほんご を べんきょうします (neru mae ni nihongo o benkyō shimasu). Before sleeping, I study Japanese.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Particles used: は for the topic (わたし, ごご, よる), に for clock time and for まえ に, を for the object of たべる, みる and べんきょうする, から after the て-form for \"after\", で for the place of action, が for the destination in がっこう に. Note that まいにち takes no particle at all, and あまり forces the negative みません.</p>", "tag": "medium"}, {"q": "Your mock test shows you finish the grammar questions but run out of time on reading. Diagnose the likely cause and give three specific fixes.", "a": "<p>The likely cause is decoding speed, not knowledge. If you know the grammar well enough to finish those questions, the reading loss is almost always slow kana recognition, or re-reading sentences because you translate as you go.</p>\n<p>Three fixes, in order of payoff:</p>\n<div class=\"steps\"><div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">1</span><div><p>Read aloud fifteen minutes a day from any N5 passage, at speed, without stopping to look words up. This is the only drill that directly raises kana reading speed.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">2</span><div><p>Change your reading order: read the question first, then find the verb of each clause, then the time and place words, then answer. Do not translate the whole passage into English.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><span class=\"step-n\">3</span><div><p>Practise with a timer on single passages, five minutes each, and stop when the timer stops whether or not you are done. Speed only improves under a clock.</p></div></div></div>\n<p>One thing not to do: do not respond by learning more grammar. The problem is throughput, and more grammar does not fix throughput. Also worth logging in your error book, so you can see whether the loss is speed or specific question types such as the underlined-reference items.</p>", "tag": "hard"}, {"q": "A classmate says: the N5 pass mark is a fixed percentage and there are exactly so many listening questions, so I only need to memorise those. How do you answer them, and what do you actually need to know?", "a": "<p>Answer honestly: those numbers are set by the exam body, they are published in the official JLPT information for each attempt, and they are not part of your syllabus. I should not quote a figure I have not verified, and neither should they. Ask them to check the official source rather than a senior's memory.</p>\n<p>More importantly, the strategy is wrong even if the numbers were right. Memorising question counts does nothing, because the test does not reward knowing the format, it rewards the four skills.</p>\n<div class=\"tbl-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>What they think helps</th><th>What actually helps</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>memorising the number of questions</td><td>reading kana at speed, so time is not the limit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>guessing which topics appear</td><td>covering the whole N5 scope: family, food, time, places, daily routine</td></tr>\n<tr><td>learning grammar rules without use</td><td>producing the patterns in writing and speech</td></tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n<p>What you genuinely need to know: the scope is familiar topics at N5 level, the paper covers vocabulary, grammar and reading plus listening, and the vocabulary, kanji and grammar in modules 1 to 5 of these notes are the working syllabus. Everything else is detail that the exam body publishes and updates.</p>", "tag": "exam"}], "file": null}];
